txt` — the reviewer contract +\n  the AUDIT section (Pins block stripped) + this item's additions. ONE file serves both seats;\n  nothing in it names a model, a peer, or any other gate.\n- **Ruling [A9]:** `pr-body.md` is up to date in the tree; a mechanical syncs the GitHub\n  pull-request body from the file after this sitting's close push (an out-of-tree act; its\n  result is in the sitting's completion report).\n\n## What completes the NEXT phase (the audit)\n\n1. The conductor verifies this push landed (ls-remote tip equals the head the sitting\n   reported).\n2. The conductor spawns TWO reviewer-runners (background, sonnet, no isolation) — one per seat\n   of the AUDIT block in `.claude/skills/work/reviewers.md`, pins exactly as that block states\n   them. Both seats use the same prompt file, `loop/items/AI4DEV-59/audit-prompt.txt`, pinned\n   to this head. Outputs land ONLY in `loop/items/AI4DEV-59/artifacts/`, seat-labelled.\n3. The phase is complete when BOTH runners report LANDED with distillates. An empty or\n   progress-line-only output is never a clean gate — hand it down as an anomaly.\n4. BOTH seats clean → spawn the MERGE sitting; it absorbs the audit wait and records both\n   clean verdicts among its dispositions. Findings from EITHER seat → spawn the AUDIT sitting,\n   which rules on BOTH readers' findings (a clean seat beside a seat with findings is\n   evidence, recorded, never a veto). If audit fixes change code, the whole panel re-runs once\n   at the new head — never one seat.\n\n## Notes for the next sitting\n\n- **Flake carry-forward:** `tests/at/harness/runner.selftest.ts` line 222, stale-lock race,\n  pre-existing on the unchanged tree (evidence: `baseline.txt` appendix). If CI goes red\n  there: one flake re-run, no new commit; the same failure again → classify pre-existing on\n  main with the baseline appendix as proof — the founder's, never fixed in this branch. Also\n  a candidate standalone item to file.\n- **Candidate item to file:** the CLI ignoring `[auth.rate_limit] email_sent` on the local\n  stack (measured this item, recorded in `stack-up.txt`) may surprise a future leaf. Not this\n  item's defect.\n- **Noted by the draft sitting, still untouched:** `tests/at/suites/req-001/_bind.ts` line\n  31's \"33 not-yet-landed ids\" count was stale before this item (baseline truth 30; after\n  this item 28). Outside the plan's surfaces; reported, not fixed.\n- **What the green does and does not claim:** `plan.md` section 4 — repeat it in the merge\n  ruling.\n\n## Open questions for the founder\n\nNone. Nothing contradicts ratified text; no scope growth.","lineStart":1,"lineEnd":73,"totalLines":73,"truncated":false}},"title":"loop\\items\\AI4DEV-59\\PHASE-STATE.md","time":{"start":1786290389109,"end":1786290389123}},"id":"prt_fe7343f8e001eVosNNRVkE2ahv","sessionID":"ses_018cbdef8ffeIiDUqdR6dh2IlP","messageID":"msg_fe7342274001LsA5UmnoZvxp7a"}}
C:\Users\nirdr\Downloads\ai4good\.claude\worktrees\agent-aa95110cbeab31aa7\loop\items\AI4DEV-59\artifacts\audit-flash.events.jsonl:6:{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1786290395229,"sessionID":"ses_018cbdef8ffeIiDUqdR6dh2IlP","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_00_R88P3oJepUbm7eva1K6l7065","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"C:\\Users\\nirdr\\Downloads\\ai4good\\.claude\\worktrees\\agent-aa95110cbeab31aa7\\loop\\items\\AI4DEV-59\\pr-body.md"},"output":"<path>C:\\Users\\nirdr\\Downloads\\ai4good\\.claude\\worktrees\\agent-aa95110cbeab31aa7\\loop\\items\\AI4DEV-59\\pr-body.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: # AI4DEV-59 — Email verification, and the gate on unverified writing\n2: \n3: Deliverable D2 leaf L1 of the authentication requirement (`loop/decomp/req-001.md`, revision\n4: `0579425`): the email-verification flow for every email-capable account type, and the gate that\n5: stops an email-unverified account writing Discovery messages.\n6: \n7: **Acceptance ids this leaf owns:** AT-001.09, AT-001.10\n8: (`.taskmaster/docs/acceptance/at-req-001.md`). It builds on the accounts leaf and the GitHub\n9: leaf (pull requests #47 and #48), which landed signup, sign-in and the account types this flow\n10: verifies.\n11: \n12: **Status: built, reviewed twice, fixes applied.** The plan is at\n13: `loop/items/AI4DEV-59/plan.md`. Two external review gates have run and both are ruled. The plan\n14: gate raised four findings; the draft-code gate was a panel of two blind seats and raised twelve.\n15: All sixteen are accepted — several fixed differently from the remedy the reviewer proposed — and\n16: every disposition, with the finding quoted verbatim, is in the plan's rulings sections 7 and 8.\n17: The fixes are applied and the live-stack proof is run. Still ahead of merge: the read-only audit,\n18: and continuous integration green on the exact head. The merge ruling — what was built, every\n19: review finding and its disposition, and exactly what the green does and does not claim, pinned to\n20: the head it licenses — will be posted on this pull request before merge.\n21: \n22: **The shape, in one paragraph.** \"Verified\" is Supabase Auth's own fact\n23: (`email_confirmed_at`); this item ships no migration. The local auth config turns email\n24: confirmations on — the change the suite's own header reserves for this leaf. The gate ships as\n25: a shared decision module (`supabase/functions/_shared/verification.ts`) that the future\n26: Discovery send route must call; no Discovery surface is built here, and no green from this item\n27: may be read as \"Discovery messaging is gated in production\". The two acceptance tests go from\n28: declared-pending stubs to real bodies at the loop tier (9 green / 28 declared red for the\n29: requirement), and a live local-stack transcript carries the evidence the loop tier cannot\n30: reach: the real confirmation email, the link flipping the account to verified, and sign-in\n31: refused before confirmation.\n\n(End of file - total 31 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"# AI4DEV-59 — Email verification, and the gate on unverified writing\n\nDeliverable D2 leaf L1 of the authentication requirement (`loop/decomp/req-001.md`, revision\n`0579425`): the email-verification flow for every email-capable account type, and the gate that\nstops an email-unverified account writing Discovery messages.\n\n**Acceptance ids this leaf owns:** AT-001.09, AT-001.10\n(`.taskmaster/docs/acceptance/at-req-001.md`). It builds on the accounts leaf and the GitHub\nleaf (pull requests #47 and #48), which landed signup, sign-in and the account types this flow\nverifies.\n\n**Status: built, reviewed twice, fixes applied.** The plan is at\n`loop/items/AI4DEV-59/plan.md`. Two external review gates have run and both are ruled. The plan\ngate raised four findings; the draft-code gate was a panel of two blind seats and raised twelve.\nAll sixteen are accepted — several fixed differently from the remedy the reviewer proposed — and\nevery disposition, with the finding quoted verbatim, is in the plan's rulings sections 7 and 8.\nThe fixes are applied and the live-stack proof is run. Still ahead of merge: the read-only audit,\nand continuous integration green on the exact head. The merge ruling — what was built, every\nreview finding and its disposition, and exactly what the green does and does not claim, pinned to\nthe head it licenses — will be posted on this pull request before merge.","truncated":false,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"C:\\Users\\nirdr\\Downloads\\ai4good\\.claude\\worktrees\\agent-aa95110cbeab31aa7\\loop\\items\\AI4DEV-59\\pr-body.md","text":"# AI4DEV-59 — Email verification, and the gate on unverified writing\n\nDeliverable D2 leaf L1 of the authentication requirement (`loop/decomp/req-001.md`, revision\n`0579425`): the email-verification flow for every email-capable account type, and the gate that\nstops an email-unverified account writing Discovery messages.\n\n**Acceptance ids this leaf owns:** AT-001.09, AT-001.10\n(`.taskmaster/docs/acceptance/at-req-001.md`). It builds on the accounts leaf and the GitHub\nleaf (pull requests #47 and #48), which landed signup, sign-in and the account types this flow\nverifies.\n\n**Status: built, reviewed twice, fixes applied.** The plan is at\n`loop/items/AI4DEV-59/plan.md`. Two external review gates have run and both are ruled. The plan\ngate raised four findings; the draft-code gate was a panel of two blind seats and raised twelve.\nAll sixteen are accepted — several fixed differently from the remedy the reviewer proposed — and\nevery disposition, with the finding quoted verbatim, is in the plan's rulings sections 7 and 8.\nThe fixes are applied and the live-stack proof is run. Still ahead of merge: the read-only audit,\nand continuous integration green on the exact head. The merge ruling — what was built, every\nreview finding and its disposition, and exactly what the green does and does not claim, pinned to\nthe head it licenses — will be posted on this pull request before merge.\n\n**The shape, in one paragraph.** \"Verified\" is Supabase Auth's own fact\n(`email_confirmed_at`); this item ships no migration. The local auth config turns email\nconfirmations on — the change the suite's own header reserves for this leaf. The gate ships as\na shared decision module (`supabase/functions/_shared/verification.ts`) that the future\nDiscovery send route must call; no Discovery surface is built here, and no green from this item\nmay be read as \"Discovery messaging is gated in production\". The two acceptance tests go from\ndeclared-pending stubs to real bodies at the loop tier (9 green / 28 declared red for the\nrequirement), and a live local-stack transcript carries the evidence the loop tier cannot\nreach: the real confirmation email, the link flipping the account to verified, and sign-in\nrefused before confirmation.","lineStart":1,"lineEnd":31,"totalLines":31,"truncated":false}},"title":"loop\\items\\AI4DEV-59\\pr-body.md","time":{"start":1786290395215,"end":1786290395227}},"id":"prt_fe734574f001XgTvvMeUPTceDP","sessionID":"ses_018cbdef8ffeIiDUqdR6dh2IlP","messageID":"msg_fe73445270019yfmL1VZxizM0z"}}
C:\Users\nirdr\Downloads\ai4good\.claude\worktrees\agent-aa95110cbeab31aa7\loop\items\AI4DEV-59\artifacts\audit-flash.events.jsonl:7:{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1786290395510,"sessionID":"ses_018cbdef8ffeIiDUqdR6dh2IlP","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_01_8Z5BrHUISz4UC4COq7Ge9582","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"C:\\Users\\nirdr\\Downloads\\ai4good\\.claude\\worktrees\\agent-aa95110cbeab31aa7\\loop\\items\\AI4DEV-59\\proof-local.ts"},"output":"<path>C:\\Users\\nirdr\\Downloads\\ai4good\\.claude\\worktrees\\agent-aa95110cbeab31aa7\\loop\\items\\AI4DEV-59\\proof-local.ts</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: /**\n2:  * THE LOCAL PROOF for AI4DEV-59 — the only evidence in this item about the real Supabase Auth\n3:  * under `enable_confirmations = true`, the real confirmation email, and the real\n4:  * `/auth/v1/user` serialisation the shipped extractor has to read.\n5:  *\n6:  *   bun loop/items/AI4DEV-59/proof-local.ts\n7:  *\n8:  * Preconditions, and the script refuses rather than guesses if any is missing:\n9:  *   1. `bun run db:start` — the stack is up, WITH this item's `supabase/config.toml`. The flip to\n10:  *      `enable_confirmations = true` is read at Auth start-up, so a stack started before the flip\n11:  *      is running the old setting and every check below would measure the wrong system.\n12:  *   2. `bun run db:reset` — run it immediately before this script. The checks assert row counts.\n13:  *   3. `bunx supabase functions serve` — running in another terminal, FROM THIS WORKTREE, WITHOUT\n14:  *      --no-verify-jwt. The directory matters and is not pedantry: the edge runtime container\n15:  *      mounts whichever project directory launched it, and this machine was once found serving a\n16:  *      worktree that had since been deleted. A stale mount answers preflights perfectly while\n17:  *      running somebody else's code. The script probes the function before using it.\n18:  *\n19:  * IT LIVES IN THE ITEM RECORD AND NOT IN `tests/`, DELIBERATELY, for the reason the predecessor's\n20:  * copy of this file gives: it is evidence-gathering. It runs once, on one machine, by hand, and\n21:  * its transcript is the artifact. Under `tests/` it would look like something that guards the\n22:  * tree, and it guards nothing — nothing runs it again and CI has no database.\n23:  *\n24:  * WHAT THE LOOP-TIER SUITE ALREADY PROVES, so this does not duplicate it: that the shipped\n25:  * decisions in `_shared/verification.ts` behave as AT-001.09 and AT-001.10 require, including the\n26:  * fail-closed answers, which `tests/at/harness/shipped-verification.selftest.ts` drives directly.\n27:  * What NOTHING BUT THIS SCRIPT touches: that GoTrue issues no session at signup with confirmations\n28:  * on, that it refuses sign-in until the address is confirmed, that a confirmation email really\n29:  * arrives and its link really flips `email_confirmed_at`, and that the shipped extractor answers\n30:  * true against GoTrue's REAL serialisation rather than against the fixture's prediction of it.\n31:  *\n32:  * ============================================================================================\n33:  * NO CHECK HERE EXERCISES `discoveryMessageAllowed`, AND THAT IS NOT AN OVERSIGHT.\n34:  * ============================================================================================\n35:  *\n36:  * There is no Discovery route on this stack or on any stack — it is REQ-002/004's, and this leaf\n37:  * ships the decision that route must call rather than the route. So there is nothing live to send\n38:  * a Discovery message to. The gate's oracle is the loop-tier body for AT-001.10 plus the direct\n39:  * shape selftest, and this transcript says so instead of leaving a reader to assume a live check\n40:  * happened. What this script DOES add on that subject is check (c): on the live stack an\n41:  * unconfirmed user cannot authenticate at all, so the auth layer blocks upstream of the gate.\n42:  *\n43:  * NO KEY IS WRITTEN INTO THIS FILE AND NONE IS PRINTED BY IT. Everything is read from\n44:  * `bunx supabase status -o json` at run time. Redaction is DESIGNED IN rather than applied\n45:  * afterwards, and it covers THREE surfaces, because gate 2 found the first version covering only\n46:  * one:\n47:  *\n48:  *   1. BODIES. `redact()` below walks every object body before it reaches the transcript and\n49:  *      replaces the value under any credential-shaped key name.\n50:  *   2. STRINGS, including a body that is not JSON at all. `redact()` also scrubs JWT-shaped\n51:  *      substrings and `name=value` pairs whose name is credential-shaped, so a raw string answer\n52:  *      cannot carry a token through verbatim.\n53:  *   3. THE REDIRECT LOCATION. `redactedLocation()` cuts the URL fragment — that fragment is where\n54:  *      GoTrue puts the access token it mints when a link is followed — AND replaces EVERY\n55:  *      query-parameter VALUE, keeping the parameter names. A `?token=` or `?code=` redirect can\n56:  *      therefore not land in a file that gets pushed.\n57:  *\n58:  * GitHub push protection has already refused one push in this project for a transcript that\n59:  * captured a status command verbatim. The transcript is ALSO inspected for credential residue by\n60:  * hand before it is committed; that inspection is recorded in the item's report.\n61:  */\n62: \n63: import { readFileSync } from 'node:fs';\n64: import { SQL } from 'bun';\n65: // THE SHIPPED EXTRACTOR, IMPORTED RATHER THAN MIRRORED — the whole point of check (d). The\n66: // question this script exists to settle is whether the function that ships answers correctly\n67: // about a REAL GoTrue response, so re-implementing the read here would settle nothing.\n68: import { emailVerifiedFromUser } from '../../../supabase/functions/_shared/verification.ts';\n69: // The stub import fixture, for the volunteer completion read-back in (e) — the same reason the\n70: // predecessor imports it: comparing the stored profile against the shipped judgement shows the\n71: // values travelled from the edge function into the transaction unchanged, where \"the row is\n72: // non-empty\" would pass over plausible-looking rubbish.\n73: import { stubGithubStatsFor } from '../../../supabase/functions/_shared/github.ts';\n74: \n75: /* ------------------------------------------------------------------------ reporting machinery */\n76: \n77: type Outcome = 'pass' | 'fail' | 'skip';\n78: type Check = { id: string; title: string; outcome: Outcome; note: string };\n79: \n80: const results: Check[] = [];\n81: \n82: function record(id: string, title: string, passed: boolean, note: string): void {\n83:   results.push({ id, title, outcome: passed ? 'pass' : 'fail', note });\n84:   console.log(`${passed ? 'PASS' : 'FAIL'}  (${id}) ${title}\\n        ${note}`);\n85: }\n86: \n87: /**\n88:  * A check that could not be attempted. Never a pass and never a failure — a skip says the evidence\n89:  * is MISSING, which is a different statement from the claim being false, and both differ from the\n90:  * claim being proved.\n91:  */\n92: function skip(id: string, title: string, why: string): void {\n93:   results.push({ id, title, outcome: 'skip', note: `SKIPPED — ${why}` });\n94:   console.log(`SKIP  (${id}) ${title}\\n        ${why}`);\n95: }\n96: \n97: function fail(message: string): never {\n98:   console.error(`\\nPRECONDITION FAILED: ${message}`);\n99:   process.exit(2);\n100: }\n101: \n102: /**\n103:  * EVERY BODY GOES THROUGH THIS BEFORE IT REACHES THE TRANSCRIPT.\n104:  *\n105:  * The fields that carry secrets are known and few — GoTrue answers with `access_token`,\n106:  * `refresh_token`, `provider_token` and friends — but the rule here is by NAME PATTERN rather than\n107:  * by a list, because a list is a thing to forget to extend. A value under any key whose name looks\n108:  * like a credential is replaced; the KEY still appears, so the transcript still shows that the\n109:  * field was present, which is usually the fact a check needs.\n110:  *\n111:  * It returns a STRING, not an object, so there is no path by which an unredacted value reaches the\n112:  * output through some later `JSON.stringify`.\n113:  */\n114: const SENSITIVE = /token|secret|password|apikey|api_key|jwt|nonce|otp|code$/i;\n115: \n116: /**\n117:  * A STRING SCRUBBED OF CREDENTIALS, for the surface `redact()`'s key walk cannot see.\n118:  *\n119:  * A key walk protects `{\"access_token\": \"...\"}`. It protects nothing inside a value — and GoTrue\n120:  * answers with raw strings on some paths, while any body at all can carry a link with a token in\n121:  * its query. So two shapes are replaced here:\n122:  *\n123:  *   - A JWT. Three base64url runs separated by dots, beginning `eyJ`, which is what a JSON header\n124:  *     always encodes to. Every access token, refresh token and service key in this stack is one.\n125:  *   - A `name=value` pair whose NAME is credential-shaped by the same pattern the key walk uses.\n126:  *     The name survives, so the transcript still shows which parameter was present.\n127:  */\n128: function scrubString(text: string): string {\n129:   return text\n130:     .replace(/eyJ[A-Za-z0-9_-]+\\.[A-Za-z0-9_-]+\\.[A-Za-z0-9_-]+/g, '<redacted-jwt>')\n131:     .replace(/([A-Za-z0-9_.-]+)=([^&\\s\"'<>]+)/g, (whole, name: string) =>\n132:       SENSITIVE.test(name) ? `${name}=<redacted>` : whole,\n133:     );\n134: }\n135: \n136: function redact(value: unknown): string {\n137:   const walk = (node: unknown): unknown => {\n138:     if (Array.isArray(node)) return node.map(walk);\n139:     if (node !== null && typeof node === 'object') {\n140:       const out: Record<string, unknown> = {};\n141:       for (const [key, inner] of Object.entries(node as Record<string, unknown>)) {\n142:         out[key] = SENSITIVE.test(key) ? '<redacted>' : walk(inner);\n143:       }\n144:       return out;\n145:     }\n146:     // A STRING VALUE IS SCRUBBED TOO. This is the half gate 2 found missing: a body that is not\n147:     // JSON reaches here as one long string, and a key walk never looks inside it.\n148:     if (typeof node === 'string') return scrubString(node);\n149:     return node;\n150:   };\n151:   try {\n152:     return JSON.stringify(walk(value));\n153:   } catch {\n154:     return '<unserialisable>';\n155:   }\n156: }\n157: \n158: /**\n159:  * A URL SAFE TO PRINT: no fragment, and no query-parameter VALUES.\n160:  *\n161:  * The fragment is where GoTrue puts the minted access token. The QUERY is where it puts the\n162:  * token hash, the `code`, and the error description — and printing those unchanged was the defect\n163:  * two blind reviewers found independently. Parameter NAMES are kept, because which parameters a\n164:  * redirect carried is usually the fact a check needs, and a name is not a credential.\n165:  */\n166: function redactedLocation(url: string): string {\n167:   if (url === '') return '';\n168:   const hash = url.indexOf('#');\n169:   const fragmentNote = hash < 0 ? '' : '#<redacted-fragment>';\n170:   const withoutHash = hash < 0 ? url : url.slice(0, hash);\n171: \n172:   const question = withoutHash.indexOf('?');\n173:   if (question < 0) return `${withoutHash}${fragmentNote}`;\n174: \n175:   const query = withoutHash\n176:     .slice(question + 1)\n177:     .split('&')\n178:     .map((pair) => {\n179:       const equals = pair.indexOf('=');\n180:       // A bare flag has no value to redact.\n181:       return equals < 0 ? pair : `${pair.slice(0, equals)}=<redacted>`;\n182:     })\n183:     .join('&');\n184:   return `${withoutHash.slice(0, question)}?${query}${fragmentNote}`;\n185: }\n186: \n187: /* --------------------------------------------------------------------------- the environment */\n188: \n189: const status = await (async () => {\n190:   const proc = Bun.spawnSync(['bunx', 'supabase', 'status', '-o', 'json'], { stdout: 'pipe', stderr: 'pipe' });\n191:   const text = new TextDecoder().decode(proc.stdout);\n192:   const start = text.indexOf('{');\n193:   // The raw text is NOT included in this message, deliberately: it is the whole status payload and\n194:   // it carries key material. A failure here says what to do, not what it read.\n195:   if (start < 0) fail('could not read `supabase status -o json` — is the stack up? (bun run db:start)');\n196:   return JSON.parse(text.slice(start)) as Record<string, string>;\n197: })();\n198: \n199: const API_URL = status.API_URL ?? fail('supabase status reported no API_URL');\n200: const DB_URL = status.DB_URL ?? fail('supabase status reported no DB_URL');\n201: /** The browser's key. Everything a signed-out or signed-in visitor could do uses this and no other. */\n202: const PUBLIC_KEY = status.ANON_KEY ?? status.PUBLISHABLE_KEY ?? fail('supabase status reported no client key');\n203: /** The server's key. Used ONLY where the check is about an authority the public never holds. */\n204: const SECRET_KEY = status.SERVICE_ROLE_KEY ?? status.SECRET_KEY ?? fail('supabase status reported no service key');\n205: /**\n206:  * The local mail catcher. Its port is 54324 in `supabase/config.toml`, and its API SHAPE is\n207:  * measured below rather than assumed — see `discoverMailCatcher`.\n208:  */\n209: const MAIL_URL = status.MAILPIT_URL ?? status.INBUCKET_URL ?? 'http://127.0.0.1:54324';\n210: \n211: const sql = new SQL(DB_URL);\n212: \n213: const TEXT_VERSION = 'tos-2026-01+promise-2026-01';\n214: const PASSWORD = 'correct horse battery staple';\n215: const CLIENT_IP = '203.0.113.7';\n216: const VOLUNTEER_HANDLE = 'octoverifier';\n217: /** Unique per run, so a second run against a database that was not reset still reads honestly. */\n218: const RUN = Date.now().toString(36);\n219: const address = (local: string) => `${local}+${RUN}@example.test`;\n220: \n221: /**\n222:  * The configured per-hour cap on auth emails, read from the file rather than remembered.\n223:  *\n224:  * This run sends TWO confirmation emails — one per email-capable account type — and the checked-in\n225:  * value is 2, so the file's cap sits EXACTLY on what this run sends — which is why check (f)\n226:  * exists and why the plan pre-authorized raising it.\n227:  *\n228:  * MEASURED ON THE FIRST RUN: the CLI did NOT push this file value into the auth container — the\n229:  * container carried GOTRUE_RATE_LIMIT_EMAIL_SENT=360000, recorded in stack-up.txt beside the\n230:  * reading — so the file's cap did not bind that run. Check (f) still reports the file's value AND\n231:  * whether any mailer refusal appeared, which together are the honest pair: the transcript shows\n232:  * what the file claims and what the mailer actually did.\n233:  */\n234: const CONFIGURED_EMAIL_RATE_LIMIT = (() => {\n235:   try {\n236:     const toml = readFileSync(new URL('../../../supabase/config.toml', import.meta.url), 'utf8');\n237:     return /^\\s*email_sent\\s*=\\s*(\\d+)/m.exec(toml)?.[1] ?? '<not found>';\n238:   } catch {\n239:     return '<unreadable>';\n240:   }\n241: })();\n242: \n243: /**\n244:  * Anything that looks like the mailer refusing to send. Check (f) reports on this, so a starved\n245:  * run can never look like a clean one.\n246:  */\n247: const rateLimitEvidence: string[] = [];\n248: \n249: function noteIfRateLimited(where: string, httpStatus: number, body: unknown): void {\n250:   const text = typeof body === 'string' ? body : JSON.stringify(body ?? null);\n251:   if (httpStatus === 429 || /rate.?limit|over_email_send/i.test(text)) {\n252:     rateLimitEvidence.push(`${where}: HTTP ${httpStatus} ${redact(body)}`);\n253:   }\n254: }\n255: \n256: /* --------------------------------------------------------------------------------- Auth calls */\n257: \n258: type Wire = { status: number; body: unknown };\n259: \n260: async function authPost(path: string, body: unknown, key = PUBLIC_KEY): Promise<Wire> {\n261:   const response = await fetch(`${API_URL}${path}`, {\n262:     method: 'POST',\n263:     headers: { 'content-type': 'application/json', apikey: key, Authorization: `Bearer ${key}` },\n264:     body: JSON.stringify(body),\n265:   });\n266:   const text = await response.text();\n267:   let parsed: unknown = text;\n268:   try {\n269:     parsed = JSON.parse(text);\n270:   } catch {\n271:     // A non-JSON answer is itself evidence; it is kept as the raw string.\n272:   }\n273:   return { status: response.status, body: parsed };\n274: }\n275: \n276: /**\n277:  * Register an email/password user. WITH CONFIRMATIONS ON THIS MUST RETURN NO SESSION, which is\n278:  * check (a)'s first half, so the caller inspects the wire response rather than being handed a\n279:  * tidy session object.\n280:  *\n281:  * THE USER ID IS TAKEN FROM THE RESPONSE WHEN IT IS THERE, AND FROM THE DATABASE WHEN IT IS NOT.\n282:  * GoTrue's confirmations-on signup response has varied across versions — some return the full user\n283:  * record, some return an obfuscated one to avoid leaking whether an address already exists. Which\n284:  * one this stack does is recorded rather than assumed, and neither answer is a failure: the id is\n285:  * an operator read either way.\n286:  */\n287: async function signUp(email: string): Promise<{ wire: Wire; userId: string; idFrom: 'response' | 'database' }> {\n288:   const wire = await authPost('/auth/v1/signup', { email, password: PASSWORD });\n289:   noteIfRateLimited(`signup ${email}`, wire.status, wire.body);\n290:   if (wire.status >= 400) fail(`sign-up for ${email} failed (HTTP ${wire.status}): ${redact(wire.body)}`);\n291: \n292:   const body = wire.body as { id?: string; user?: { id?: string } } | null;\n293:   const fromResponse = body?.user?.id ?? body?.id;\n294:   if (typeof fromResponse === 'string' && fromResponse !== '') {\n295:     return { wire, userId: fromResponse, idFrom: 'response' };\n296:   }\n297:   const rows = await sql`select id from auth.users where email = ${email}`;\n298:   if (rows.length !== 1) fail(`sign-up for ${email} left ${rows.length} rows in auth.users — expected exactly 1`);\n299:   return { wire, userId: String(rows[0].id), idFrom: 'database' };\n300: }\n301: \n302: /** Sign in with a password. Returns the wire answer AND the token, which is never printed. */\n303: async function signIn(email: string): Promise<{ wire: Wire; accessToken: string | null }> {\n304:   const wire = await authPost('/auth/v1/token?grant_type=password', { email, password: PASSWORD });\n305:   const token = (wire.body as { access_token?: string } | null)?.access_token;\n306:   return { wire, accessToken: typeof token === 'string' && token !== '' ? token : null };\n307: }\n308: \n309: /**\n310:  * GoTrue's error code for \"this address is not confirmed yet\".\n311:  *\n312:  * It is the DISCRIMINATOR check (c) needs, and it is pinned as a constant so the transcript states\n313:  * which value was expected. If the live stack answers a different code, the check FAILS and the\n314:  * verbatim capture beside it shows the real one — measurement, never assumption.\n315:  */\n316: const CONFIRMATION_ERROR_CODE = 'email_not_confirmed';\n317: \n318: /**\n319:  * IS THIS THE CONFIRMATION REFUSAL, or merely A refusal — the distinction gate 2 found missing.\n320:  *\n321:  * `status >= 400` alone passes on a 429 from the mailer's rate limiter and on a 500 from a broken\n322:  * stack, and either would record \"confirmation is enforced\" while confirmation enforced nothing.\n323:  * So a pass needs BOTH halves:\n324:  *\n325:  *   - a 4xx that is NOT 429 — a client refusal, not a throttle and not a server fault;\n326:  *   - a body that NAMES the unconfirmed state — the pinned error code, or `confirm` in the\n327:  *     message, which is the weaker initial predicate the ruling allows.\n328:  *\n329:  * IT READS THE IN-MEMORY BODY, NOT THE PRINTED ONE, and that is load-bearing: the transcript\n330:  * redactor's key pattern ends with `code$`, so `error_code` is blanked in everything it prints.\n331:  * Judging the printed text would judge `<redacted>`. The discriminator is surfaced separately\n332:  * below through a safe extraction — an error code is an enum value, not a credential.\n333:  */\n334: function unconfirmedSignInRefusal(wire: Wire): { ok: boolean; discriminator: string } {\n335:   const body =\n336:     wire.body !== null && typeof wire.body === 'object'\n337:       ? (wire.body as Record<string, unknown>)\n338:       : {};\n339:   const errorCode = typeof body.error_code === 'string' ? body.error_code : '';\n340:   const message = [body.msg, body.message, body.error_description, body.error, wire.body]\n341:     .filter((part): part is string => typeof part === 'string')\n342:     .join(' | ');\n343: \n344:   const statusIsClientRefusal = wire.status >= 400 && wire.status < 500 && wire.status !== 429;\n345:   const bodyNamesTheState = errorCode === CONFIRMATION_ERROR_CODE || /confirm/i.test(message);\n346: \n347:   return {\n348:     ok: statusIsClientRefusal && bodyNamesTheState,\n349:     discriminator:\n350:       `HTTP ${wire.status} · error_code=${errorCode === '' ? '<absent>' : errorCode}` +\n351:       ` (expected ${CONFIRMATION_ERROR_CODE}) · message=${JSON.stringify(scrubString(message) || '<absent>')}`,\n352:   };\n353: }\n354: \n355: /**\n356:  * THE SAME LINK WITH A TOKEN GOTRUE NEVER ISSUED — check (b2)'s instrument.\n357:  *\n358:  * The mirror under test says a link that was never issued confirms nothing, and following a\n359:  * MUTATED token is how that is measured without touching any semantics REQ-001 does not state.\n360:  * The token this returns was never minted, so following it says nothing about expiry, about single\n361:  * use, or about resend — retired AT-001.11 stays untouched. Every other part of the link, the\n362:  * `type` and the redirect included, is left exactly as GoTrue wrote it, so the only difference\n363:  * between this request and the real one is the token itself.\n364:  *\n365:  * The mutation shifts digits and letters inside their own alphabet, which keeps the token the same\n366:  * length and shape. A token that changed shape could be refused for being malformed rather than\n367:  * for being unissued, and the check would then measure input validation instead of the mirror.\n368:  */\n369: function tamperVerificationToken(link: string): { tampered: string; parameter: string } | null {\n370:   let url: URL;\n371:   try {\n372:     url = new URL(link);\n373:   } catch {\n374:     return null;\n375:   }\n376:   const parameter = ['token', 'token_hash', 'confirmation_token'].find((name) => {\n377:     const value = url.searchParams.get(name);\n378:     return typeof value === 'string' && value !== '';\n379:   });\n380:   if (parameter === undefined) return null;\n381: \n382:   const original = url.searchParams.get(parameter) as string;\n383:   const mutated = original.replace(/[0-9a-zA-Z]/g, (character) => {\n384:     if (character >= '0' && character <= '9') return String((Number(character) + 5) % 10);\n385:     const base = character >= 'a' ? 'a'.charCodeAt(0) : 'A'.charCodeAt(0);\n386:     return String.fromCharCode(base + ((character.charCodeAt(0) - base + 13) % 26));\n387:   });\n388:   if (mutated === original) return null;\n389: \n390:   url.searchParams.set(parameter, mutated);\n391:   return { tampered: url.toString(), parameter };\n392: }\n393: \n394: /** `auth.users.email_confirmed_at` read as the operator, on port 54322 — not through any API. */\n395: async function confirmedAt(userId: string): Promise<string | null> {\n396:   const rows = await sql`select email_confirmed_at from auth.users where id = ${userId}::uuid`;\n397:   const value = rows[0]?.email_confirmed_at ?? null;\n398:   return value === null ? null : String(value instanceof Date ? value.toISOString() : value);\n399: }\n400: \n401: /**\n402:  * THE POST-LINK AUTH STATE FOR GITHUB, FABRICATED BY OPERATOR AUTHORITY — carried over from the\n403:  * predecessor item unchanged, including the correction that cost it real time.\n404:  *\n405:  * Linking a GitHub identity is an OAuth consent round trip: a person at github.com and a redirect\n406:  * no script can drive. What linking LEAVES BEHIND is a row in `auth.identities`, and that row is\n407:  * what the shipped code reads. So the row is written directly, as the operator. IT IS\n408:  * FLIP-INDEPENDENT — a database write, not an auth flow — which is why (e) can reuse it under the\n409:  * new setting without re-proving it.\n410:  *\n411:  * `created_at`, `updated_at` and `last_sign_in_at` are NULLABLE in the table, so a row without them\n412:  * inserts happily; but GoTrue reads identities into a Go struct with non-pointer timestamps, so\n413:  * `GET /auth/v1/user` then answers 500 for that user, and the whole thing looks exactly like an\n414:  * expired token rather than like a malformed row three layers away. So they are filled here.\n415:  */\n416: async function fabricateGithubIdentity(userId: string, handle: string): Promise<void> {\n417:   await sql`\n418:     insert into auth.identities (id, user_id, provider, provider_id, identity_data,\n419:                                  created_at, updated_at, last_sign_in_at)\n420:     values (\n421:       gen_random_uuid(),\n422:       ${userId}::uuid,\n423:       'github',\n424:       ${`gh-${handle}-${RUN}`},\n425:       -- The casts are explicit because jsonb_build_object takes \"any\", so PostgreSQL has nothing to\n426:       -- infer a parameter's type from and refuses the statement outright rather than guessing.\n427:       jsonb_build_object('sub', ${userId}::text, 'user_name', ${handle}::text, 'provider_id', ${`gh-${handle}-${RUN}`}::text),\n428:       now(), now(), now()\n429:     )`;\n430: }\n431: \n432: /* ------------------------------------------------------------------------ the edge function */\n433: \n434: async function callFunction(name: string, accessToken: string, body: unknown): Promise<Wire> {\n435:   const response = await fetch(`${API_URL}/functions/v1/${name}`, {\n436:     method: 'POST',\n437:     headers: {\n438:       'content-type': 'application/json',\n439:       apikey: PUBLIC_KEY,\n440:       Authorization: `Bearer ${accessToken}`,\n441:       'x-forwarded-for': CLIENT_IP,\n442:     },\n443:     body: JSON.stringify(body),\n444:   });\n445:   const text = await response.text();\n446:   let parsed: unknown = text;\n447:   try {\n448:     parsed = JSON.parse(text);\n449:   } catch {\n450:     /* kept raw */\n451:   }\n452:   return { status: response.status, body: parsed };\n453: }\n454: \n455: // The stale-mount hazard the header names: probe the function before any check depends on it. An\n456: // unauthenticated POST must be REFUSED by something, and a connection error is a different failure\n457: // from a refusal. Only the second means the function is being served.\n458: //\n459: // THE PROBE GUARDS REACHABILITY, NOT REVISION, AND (e)'s EVIDENCE DOES NOT NEED IT TO.\n460: // A reviewer read this as a hole: a stale mount serving an older worktree answers 401, so any\n461: // non-404 passes and the probe cannot tell which REVISION of `complete-signup` replied. The fact is\n462: // right; the consequence is not, and the reason was verified in the tree before this comment was\n463: // written. This branch changes NOTHING under `supabase/functions/` except the NEW\n464: // `_shared/verification.ts` (`git diff main...HEAD --stat -- supabase/functions/`: one file, 167\n465: // insertions), and `complete-signup/index.ts` imports only `_shared/accounts.ts`,\n466: // `_shared/github.ts` and `_shared/edge.ts` — never `verification.ts`. So every revision of\n467: // `complete-signup` this branch could be compared against is byte for byte the same code, and the\n468: // mount's revision is not load-bearing for anything (e) claims.\n469: // The operator side of the binding is recorded rather than inferred:\n470: // `loop/items/AI4DEV-59/stack-up.txt` carries the exact `bunx supabase functions serve` command and\n471: // the directory it ran in.\n472: await (async () => {\n473:   try {\n474:     const probe = await fetch(`${API_URL}/functions/v1/complete-signup`, {\n475:       method: 'POST',\n476:       headers: { 'content-type': 'application/json', apikey: PUBLIC_KEY },\n477:       body: '{}',\n478:     });\n479:     if (probe.status === 404) {\n480:       fail('the edge runtime answered 404 for complete-signup — `bunx supabase functions serve` is not serving THIS worktree');\n481:     }\n482:   } catch (error) {\n483:     fail(\n484:       'could not reach the edge runtime at all — run `bunx supabase functions serve` from THIS worktree ' +\n485:         `(${error instanceof Error ? error.message : String(error)})`,\n486:     );\n487:   }\n488: })();\n489: \n490: /* ------------------------------------------------------------------- the mail catcher, MEASURED */\n491: \n492: type CatcherKind = 'mailpit' | 'inbucket' | 'unknown';\n493: \n494: /**\n495:  * WHICH MAIL CATCHER IS BEHIND PORT 54324, established by asking it rather than by remembering.\n496:  *\n497:  * The Supabase CLI has shipped Inbucket and, more recently, Mailpit, and their APIs are not the\n498:  * same shape. The plan's risk 2 is exactly this. So both are probed, BOTH ANSWERS GO IN THE\n499:  * TRANSCRIPT, and the extraction below branches on what actually replied. An assumption here would\n500:  * fail as \"no confirmation email arrived\", which is indistinguishable from the mailer being\n501:  * rate-limited — the single most misleading way this script could go wrong.\n502:  */\n503: async function discoverMailCatcher(): Promise<{ kind: CatcherKind; evidence: string }> {\n504:   const probe = async (path: string): Promise<string> => {\n505:     try {\n506:       const response = await fetch(`${MAIL_URL}${path}`);\n507:       const text = (await response.text()).slice(0, 200);\n508:       return `GET ${path} -> HTTP ${response.status} ${JSON.stringify(text)}`;\n509:     } catch (error) {\n510:       return `GET ${path} -> unreachable (${error instanceof Error ? error.message : String(error)})`;\n511:     }\n512:   };\n513: \n514:   const mailpit = await probe('/api/v1/info');\n515:   const inbucket = await probe('/api/v1/mailbox/probe');\n516:   const evidence = `catcher at ${MAIL_URL} · ${mailpit} · ${inbucket}`;\n517: \n518:   if (/HTTP 200/.test(mailpit)) return { kind: 'mailpit', evidence };\n519:   if (/HTTP 200/.test(inbucket)) return { kind: 'inbucket', evidence };\n520:   return { kind: 'unknown', evidence };\n521: }\n522: \n523: const catcher = await discoverMailCatcher();\n524: console.log(`\\nmail catcher shape, measured: ${catcher.kind}\\n        ${catcher.evidence}\\n`);\n525: \n526: /**\n527:  * Every URL in the message that looks like GoTrue's verify endpoint.\n528:  *\n529:  * `&amp;` IS DECODED FIRST, and that is not cosmetic: the confirmation email's HTML part escapes\n530:  * the ampersands, so a link lifted straight out of it carries `&amp;type=signup` and GoTrue reads\n531:  * the parameter name as `amp;type`. The failure looks like a rejected link rather than like a\n532:  * mangled one.\n533:  */\n534: function verifyLinksIn(text: string): string[] {\n535:   const decoded = text.replace(/&amp;/g, '&');\n536:   return decoded.match(/https?:\\/\\/[^\\s\"'<>)\\]]+\\/auth\\/v1\\/verify[^\\s\"'<>)\\]]*/g) ?? [];\n537: }\n538: \n539: async function emailedVerificationLink(email: string): Promise<{ link: string | null; evidence: string }> {\n540:   const local = email.split('@')[0];\n541:   try {\n542:     if (catcher.kind === 'mailpit') {\n543:       const list = await fetch(`${MAIL_URL}/api/v1/search?query=${encodeURIComponent(`to:${email}`)}&limit=20`);\n544:       const listed = (await list.json()) as { messages?: Array<{ ID?: string }> };\n545:       const id = listed.messages?.[0]?.ID;\n546:       if (!id) return { link: null, evidence: `mailpit search for ${email} returned no message` };\n547:       const message = await fetch(`${MAIL_URL}/api/v1/message/${id}`);\n548:       const parsed = (await message.json()) as { Text?: string; HTML?: string };\n549:       const links = verifyLinksIn(`${parsed.Text ?? ''}\\n${parsed.HTML ?? ''}`);\n550:       return {\n551:         link: links[0] ?? null,\n552:         evidence: `mailpit held ${listed.messages?.length ?? 0} message(s) for ${email}; ${links.length} verify link(s) found`,\n553:       };\n554:     }\n555:     if (catcher.kind === 'inbucket') {\n556:       const list = await fetch(`${MAIL_URL}/api/v1/mailbox/${encodeURIComponent(local)}`);\n557:       const listed = (await list.json()) as Array<{ id?: string }>;\n558:       const id = listed[0]?.id;\n559:       if (!id) return { link: null, evidence: `inbucket mailbox ${local} was empty` };\n560:       const message = await fetch(`${MAIL_URL}/api/v1/mailbox/${encodeURIComponent(local)}/${id}`);\n561:       const parsed = (await message.json()) as { body?: { text?: string; html?: string } };\n562:       const links = verifyLinksIn(`${parsed.body?.text ?? ''}\\n${parsed.body?.html ?? ''}`);\n563:       return {\n564:         link: links[0] ?? null,\n565:         evidence: `inbucket mailbox ${local} held ${listed.length} message(s); ${links.length} verify link(s) found`,\n566:       };\n567:     }\n568:     return { link: null, evidence: `the catcher at ${MAIL_URL} matched no known API shape` };\n569:   } catch (error) {\n570:     return { link: null, evidence: `reading the catcher failed: ${error instanceof Error ? error.message : String(error)}` };\n571:   }\n572: }\n573: \n574: /**\n575:  * THE FALLBACK THE PLAN'S RISK 2 PRE-AUTHORIZES, and it NARROWS the claim rather than rescuing it.\n576:  *\n577:  * GoTrue's admin endpoint mints a verification link with operator authority. If the catcher cannot\n578:  * yield the emailed link, this is what gets followed — and the transcript then says, in the check's\n579:  * own note, that the link followed was NOT the emailed one. That is a smaller claim, stated as a\n580:  * smaller claim. It is never used when the catcher works.\n581:  */\n582: async function adminGeneratedLink(email: string): Promise<{ link: string | null; evidence: string }> {\n583:   const wire = await authPost('/auth/v1/admin/generate_link', { type: 'signup', email, password: PASSWORD }, SECRET_KEY);\n584:   const link = (wire.body as { action_link?: string } | null)?.action_link ?? null;\n585:   return {\n586:     link: typeof link === 'string' && link !== '' ? link : null,\n587:     evidence: `admin generate_link -> HTTP ${wire.status} (action_link ${link ? 'present' : 'absent'})`,\n588:   };\n589: }\n590: \n591: /* ============================================================================================\n592:  * THE VERIFICATION ROUND TRIP — (a) through (d), as one function so the SECOND address in (e)\n593:  * repeats exactly the same measurement rather than an abbreviated version of it.\n594:  * ============================================================================================ */\n595: \n596: /**\n597:  * What following a tampered link did — or why it could not be followed at all.\n598:  *\n599:  * `attempted: false` is never a pass. Check (b2) SKIPS on it, because a check that could not run\n600:  * is a missing measurement rather than a satisfied one.\n601:  */\n602: type TamperedFollow = {\n603:   attempted: boolean;\n604:   why: string;\n605:   parameter: string;\n606:   status: number;\n607:   location: string;\n608:   confirmedAfter: string | null;\n609: };\n610: \n611: type RoundTrip = {\n612:   email: string;\n613:   userId: string;\n614:   idFrom: 'response' | 'database';\n615:   signupWire: Wire;\n616:   /** the whole point of (a): with confirmations on there must be NO token in the signup answer */\n617:   signupCarriedSession: boolean;\n618:   confirmedBefore: string | null;\n619:   linkSource: 'emailed' | 'admin-generated' | 'none';\n620:   linkEvidence: string;\n621:   signInBefore: Wire;\n622:   /** check (b2): the never-issued-token negative, measured BEFORE the real link is followed */\n623:   tampered: TamperedFollow;\n624:   followStatus: number;\n625:   followLocation: string;\n626:   confirmedAfter: string | null;\n627:   signInAfter: Wire;\n628:   accessToken: string | null;\n629:   rawUserWire: Wire;\n630:   shippedVerdict: boolean;\n631: };\n632: \n633: async function verificationRoundTrip(email: string): Promise<RoundTrip> {\n634:   const { wire: signupWire, userId, idFrom } = await signUp(email);\n635:   const signupBody = signupWire.body as { access_token?: unknown; session?: unknown } | null;\n636:   const signupCarriedSession =\n637:     typeof signupBody?.access_token === 'string' || (signupBody?.session !== null && signupBody?.session !== undefined);\n638: \n639:   const confirmedBefore = await confirmedAt(userId);\n640: \n641:   // (c) is measured BEFORE the link is followed, because afterwards the same call succeeds and the\n642:   // refusal would be unobtainable.\n643:   const signInBefore = (await signIn(email)).wire;\n644: \n645:   const fromCatcher = await emailedVerificationLink(email);\n646:   let link = fromCatcher.link;\n647:   let linkSource: RoundTrip['linkSource'] = link ? 'emailed' : 'none';\n648:   let linkEvidence = fromCatcher.evidence;\n649:   if (!link) {\n650:     const fallback = await adminGeneratedLink(email);\n651:     if (fallback.link) {\n652:       link = fallback.link;\n653:       linkSource = 'admin-generated';\n654:     }\n655:     linkEvidence = `${fromCatcher.evidence} · FALLBACK: ${fallback.evidence}`;\n656:   }\n657: \n658:   // (b2) THE NEVER-ISSUED TOKEN, FOLLOWED BEFORE THE REAL LINK.\n659:   //\n660:   // The order matters and is the whole design of this block: once the real link has confirmed the\n661:   // address, `email_confirmed_at` is non-null for every later read, and the negative becomes\n662:   // unmeasurable. So the tampered variant goes first, and the column is read straight afterwards.\n663:   const tampered: TamperedFollow = {\n664:     attempted: false,\n665:     why: '',\n666:     parameter: '',\n667:     status: 0,\n668:     location: '',\n669:     confirmedAfter: null,\n670:   };\n671:   if (!link) {\n672:     tampered.why = 'no verification link was obtained at all, so there was nothing to tamper with';\n673:   } else {\n674:     const mutation = tamperVerificationToken(link);\n675:     if (mutation === null) {\n676:       tampered.why = 'the link carries no non-empty token parameter this script knows how to mutate';\n677:     } else {\n678:       const followed = await fetch(mutation.tampered, { redirect: 'manual' });\n679:       tampered.attempted = true;\n680:       tampered.parameter = mutation.parameter;\n681:       tampered.status = followed.status;\n682:       tampered.location = redactedLocation(followed.headers.get('location') ?? '');\n683:       tampered.confirmedAfter = await confirmedAt(userId);\n684:     }\n685:   }\n686: \n687:   let followStatus = 0;\n688:   let followLocation = '';\n689:   if (link) {\n690:     const followed = await fetch(link, { redirect: 'manual' });\n691:     followStatus = followed.status;\n692:     // THE FRAGMENT IS CUT AND EVERY QUERY VALUE IS REPLACED. GoTrue redirects to the site URL with\n693:     // the freshly minted access token in the URL fragment, and this string goes into a file that\n694:     // gets pushed.\n695:     followLocation = redactedLocation(followed.headers.get('location') ?? '');\n696:   }\n697: \n698:   const confirmedAfter = await confirmedAt(userId);\n699:   const after = await signIn(email);\n700:   const rawUserWire: Wire = after.accessToken\n701:     ? await (async () => {\n702:         const response = await fetch(`${API_URL}/auth/v1/user`, {\n703:           headers: { apikey: PUBLIC_KEY, Authorization: `Bearer ${after.accessToken}` },\n704:         });\n705:         const text = await response.text();\n706:         let parsed: unknown = text;\n707:         try {\n708:           parsed = JSON.parse(text);\n709:         } catch {\n710:           /* kept raw */\n711:         }\n712:         return { status: response.status, body: parsed };\n713:       })()\n714:     : { status: 0, body: null };\n715: \n716:   return {\n717:     email,\n718:     userId,\n719:     idFrom,\n720:     signupWire,\n721:     signupCarriedSession,\n722:     confirmedBefore,\n723:     linkSource,\n724:     linkEvidence,\n725:     signInBefore,\n726:     tampered,\n727:     followStatus,\n728:     followLocation,\n729:     confirmedAfter,\n730:     signInAfter: after.wire,\n731:     accessToken: after.accessToken,\n732:     rawUserWire,\n733:     // THE SHIPPED FUNCTION, ON THE REAL BODY. This single line is what binds the extractor to\n734:     // GoTrue's actual serialisation; everything else in this script is the setup that makes it\n735:     // meaningful.\n736:     shippedVerdict: emailVerifiedFromUser(rawUserWire.body),\n737:   };\n738: }\n739: \n740: const ngoEmail = address('ngo-verify');\n741: const ngoTrip = await verificationRoundTrip(ngoEmail);\n742: \n743: /* =============================================================================================\n744:  * (a) SIGNUP UNDER THE FLIPPED CONFIG ISSUES NO SESSION, AND THE ADDRESS IS UNCONFIRMED.\n745:  *\n746:  * The first of the plan's three unverified-runtime-claims, measured rather than expected. Both\n747:  * halves matter: no token on the wire, AND `email_confirmed_at IS NULL` read as the operator on\n748:  * port 54322. The wire answer alone could be a client-library convention; the column is the fact.\n749:  * ============================================================================================= */\n750: \n751: record(\n752:   'a',\n753:   'an email/password signup under enable_confirmations = true returns NO session, and auth.users.email_confirmed_at is NULL',\n754:   ngoTrip.signupWire.status < 400 && ngoTrip.signupCarriedSession === false && ngoTrip.confirmedBefore === null,\n755:   `signup HTTP ${ngoTrip.signupWire.status} ${redact(ngoTrip.signupWire.body)}` +\n756:     ` · carried a session: ${ngoTrip.signupCarriedSession}` +\n757:     ` · user id read from the ${ngoTrip.idFrom}` +\n758:     ` · auth.users.email_confirmed_at = ${ngoTrip.confirmedBefore ?? 'NULL'}`,\n759: );\n760: \n761: /* =============================================================================================\n762:  * (b) THE CONFIRMATION EMAIL EXISTS IN THE CATCHER, AND THE LINK COMES OUT OF IT.\n763:  *\n764:  * The catcher's API shape was measured above, not assumed — the evidence line says which shape\n765:  * answered. If the link came from the admin endpoint instead, this check says so IN ITS OWN NOTE\n766:  * and the claim is narrower: an equivalent link was followed, but not the emailed one.\n767:  *\n768:  * AN UNRECOGNISED CATCHER SKIPS RATHER THAN FAILS, and the distinction is the shared invariant\n769:  * about re-measuring a negative: a broken instrument and a true absence produce identical output.\n770:  * \"The mailbox reads empty\" is not evidence that no email was sent when the thing being asked is\n771:  * not the thing that answers. A skip says the evidence is missing, which is what is true.\n772:  * ============================================================================================= */\n773: \n774: if (catcher.kind === 'unknown') {\n775:   skip(\n776:     'b',\n777:     'the local mail catcher holds a confirmation email for the address, and the verification link is extracted from it',\n778:     `the catcher at ${MAIL_URL} matched no known API shape, so this check has no working instrument. ` +\n779:       `It is NOT evidence that no confirmation email was sent — a broken reader and an empty mailbox look the same. ` +\n780:       `Probe results: ${catcher.evidence}. Fix the reader, or note which catcher this CLI version ships, then re-run.`,\n781:   );\n782: } else {\n783:   record(\n784:     'b',\n785:     'the local mail catcher holds a confirmation email for the address, and the verification link is extracted from it',\n786:     ngoTrip.linkSource === 'emailed',\n787:     `catcher shape: ${catcher.kind} · ${ngoTrip.linkEvidence} · link source: ${ngoTrip.linkSource}` +\n788:       (ngoTrip.linkSource === 'admin-generated'\n789:         ? ' — NARROWED: the EMAILED link was not what was followed; an operator-minted equivalent was.'\n790:         : ''),\n791:   );\n792: }\n793: \n794: /* =============================================================================================\n795:  * (b2) A TOKEN GOTRUE NEVER ISSUED CONFIRMS NOTHING.\n796:  *\n797:  * The fixture's mirror 2 says a link that was never issued has no confirming effect. Two blind\n798:  * reviewers found the same defect: that mirror was labelled BOUND by checks (a)–(d), and none of\n799:  * those checks ever follows an unissued link — the named evidence measured only the positive half.\n800:  * This check is the negative, measured.\n801:  *\n802:  * The instrument is the REAL link with its token mutated inside its own alphabet, followed BEFORE\n803:  * the real one. What it asserts is exactly one thing: `auth.users.email_confirmed_at` is still\n804:  * NULL afterwards. It asserts NOTHING about expiry, single use or resend — the token followed was\n805:  * never minted, so no lifetime and no use count is in play, and retired AT-001.11's semantics stay\n806:  * untouched.\n807:  *\n808:  * THE RESPONSE STATUS IS RECORDED BUT NOT ASSERTED ON. GoTrue may answer a redirect carrying an\n809:  * error, or a 4xx, and which one it picks is its own business. The column is the fact.\n810:  * ============================================================================================= */\n811: \n812: if (!ngoTrip.tampered.attempted) {\n813:   skip(\n814:     'b2',\n815:     'a verification link carrying a token GoTrue never issued confirms nothing',\n816:     `${ngoTrip.tampered.why}. Link source was \"${ngoTrip.linkSource}\" (${ngoTrip.linkEvidence}). ` +\n817:       'This is NOT evidence that a never-issued token confirms an address — it is evidence that the ' +\n818:       'negative could not be measured on this run.',\n819:   );\n820: } else {\n821:   record(\n822:     'b2',\n823:     'a verification link carrying a token GoTrue never issued confirms nothing',\n824:     ngoTrip.tampered.confirmedAfter === null,\n825:     `the \"${ngoTrip.tampered.parameter}\" parameter of the ${ngoTrip.linkSource} link was mutated inside its own alphabet` +\n826:       ` · following the tampered link answered HTTP ${ngoTrip.tampered.status} -> ${ngoTrip.tampered.location || '<no location header>'}` +\n827:       ` · auth.users.email_confirmed_at immediately afterwards = ${ngoTrip.tampered.confirmedAfter ?? 'NULL'}` +\n828:       ' · this says nothing about expiry, single use or resend: the token followed was never issued' +\n829:       (ngoTrip.linkSource === 'admin-generated'\n830:         ? ' · NARROWED: the link mutated was the operator-minted one, because the catcher yielded none.'\n831:         : ''),\n832:   );\n833: }\n834: \n835: /* =============================================================================================\n836:  * (c) SIGN-IN BEFORE CONFIRMATION IS REFUSED, WITH THE REFUSAL TEXT CAPTURED VERBATIM.\n837:  *\n838:  * The second unverified-runtime-claim. On the live stack this block sits UPSTREAM of the shipped\n839:  * gate: an unconfirmed user cannot authenticate at all, so it never reaches a write path. That is\n840:  * why the gate is not redundant rather than why it is — decision-8 makes verification the write\n841:  * path's own floor, and non-public paths do not pass through this refusal.\n842:  *\n843:  * THE REFUSAL MUST BE THE CONFIRMATION ONE, not any refusal. `status >= 400` was the first\n844:  * version's predicate and a reviewer showed what it accepts: a 429 from the mailer's rate limiter\n845:  * and a 500 from a broken stack both record as PASS while confirmation enforces nothing. See\n846:  * `unconfirmedSignInRefusal` above for the two halves a pass now needs.\n847:  * ============================================================================================= */\n848: \n849: const ngoRefusal = unconfirmedSignInRefusal(ngoTrip.signInBefore);\n850: \n851: record(\n852:   'c',\n853:   'sign-in BEFORE confirmation is refused by Auth itself, with a CONFIRMATION-SPECIFIC refusal (a 4xx that is not 429, naming the unconfirmed state)',\n854:   ngoRefusal.ok,\n855:   `sign-in ${ngoRefusal.discriminator}` +\n856:     ` · redacted body: ${redact(ngoTrip.signInBefore.body)} — captured verbatim, this is the refusal text` +\n857:     ' · the discriminator above is read from the in-memory body, because the redactor blanks error_code',\n858: );\n859: \n860: /* =============================================================================================\n861:  * (d) USING THE LINK FLIPS THE COLUMN, SIGN-IN THEN SUCCEEDS, AND THE SHIPPED EXTRACTOR AGREES\n862:  *     WITH GOTRUE'S REAL SERIALISATION.\n863:  *\n864:  * The third unverified-runtime-claim and the one this whole script exists for. `emailVerifiedFromUser`\n865:  * is fed the RAW `/auth/v1/user` body — not a shape this script built — and must answer true.\n866:  *\n867:  * ITS FALSE HALF IS NOT EXERCISED HERE, and the transcript says so rather than dressing it up: an\n868:  * unconfirmed user holds no session, so there is no token with which to read `/auth/v1/user` for\n869:  * one. The false half is exercised at the loop tier and in the direct shape selftest.\n870:  * ============================================================================================= */\n871: \n872: record(\n873:   'd',\n874:   'following the verification link flips email_confirmed_at, sign-in then succeeds, and the SHIPPED emailVerifiedFromUser answers true on the real /auth/v1/user body',\n875:   ngoTrip.followStatus >= 300 &&\n876:     ngoTrip.followStatus < 400 &&\n877:     ngoTrip.confirmedAfter !== null &&\n878:     ngoTrip.signInAfter.status === 200 &&\n879:     ngoTrip.accessToken !== null &&\n880:     ngoTrip.rawUserWire.status === 200 &&\n881:     ngoTrip.shippedVerdict === true,\n882:   `link follow HTTP ${ngoTrip.followStatus} -> ${ngoTrip.followLocation || '<no location header>'}` +\n883:     ` · email_confirmed_at before=${ngoTrip.confirmedBefore ?? 'NULL'} after=${ngoTrip.confirmedAfter ?? 'NULL'}` +\n884:     ` · sign-in after HTTP ${ngoTrip.signInAfter.status} (access token present: ${ngoTrip.accessToken !== null})` +\n885:     ` · /auth/v1/user HTTP ${ngoTrip.rawUserWire.status} ${redact(ngoTrip.rawUserWire.body)}` +\n886:     ` · the SHIPPED emailVerifiedFromUser says: ${ngoTrip.shippedVerdict}` +\n887:     ' · the extractor\\'s FALSE half is NOT exercised here: an unconfirmed user holds no session to read /auth/v1/user with',\n888: );\n889: \n890: /* =============================================================================================\n891:  * (e) BOTH EMAIL-CAPABLE ACCOUNT TYPES FLOW END TO END UNDER THE FLIPPED CONFIG.\n892:  *\n893:  * WHY THE PREDECESSOR'S COMPLETION EVIDENCE IS NOT CITED HERE, which is gate-1 ruling [2] adopted\n894:  * whole: the flip changes the auth context beneath BOTH completion paths. That evidence was\n895:  * produced with confirmations OFF, so it predates this change and no longer covers it. Both paths\n896:  * are therefore re-proved rather than referenced.\n897:  *\n898:  * The NGO address, already confirmed by (a)–(d), completes through the DEPLOYED complete-signup\n899:  * edge function. The volunteer address repeats the whole round trip on its own, then gains a\n900:  * GitHub identity by the operator insert above — flip-independent, because it is a database write\n901:  * and not an auth flow — and completes as a volunteer through the same deployed function.\n902:  * ============================================================================================= */\n903: \n904: const ngoCompletion = ngoTrip.accessToken\n905:   ? await callFunction('complete-signup', ngoTrip.accessToken, {\n906:       accountType: 'ngo',\n907:       organizationName: 'Riverside Shelter',\n908:       acknowledgmentTextVersion: TEXT_VERSION,\n909:     })\n910:   : { status: 0, body: 'no access token — the NGO never signed in' };\n911: \n912: const ngoRows = await sql`\n913:   select a.account_type, o.name, m.role\n914:     from public.accounts a\n915:     left join public.org_memberships m on m.account_id = a.id\n916:     left join public.organizations o on o.id = m.org_id\n917:    where a.id = ${ngoTrip.userId}::uuid`;\n918: \n919: const volunteerEmail = address('volunteer-verify');\n920: const volunteerTrip = await verificationRoundTrip(volunteerEmail);\n921: \n922: if (volunteerTrip.accessToken) await fabricateGithubIdentity(volunteerTrip.userId, VOLUNTEER_HANDLE);\n923: \n924: const volunteerCompletion = volunteerTrip.accessToken\n925:   ? await callFunction('complete-signup', volunteerTrip.accessToken, {\n926:       accountType: 'volunteer',\n927:       acknowledgmentTextVersion: TEXT_VERSION,\n928:     })\n929:   : { status: 0, body: 'no access token — the volunteer never signed in' };\n930: \n931: const volunteerAccountRows = await sql`select account_type from public.accounts where id = ${volunteerTrip.userId}::uuid`;\n932: const volunteerProfileRows = await sql`\n933:   select github_handle, top_languages, repository_count, contribution_summary, imported_at\n934:     from public.volunteer_profiles where account_id = ${volunteerTrip.userId}::uuid`;\n935: const expectedStats = stubGithubStatsFor(VOLUNTEER_HANDLE);\n936: const volunteerProfile = volunteerProfileRows[0] as\n937:   | { github_handle: string; top_languages: string[]; repository_count: number; contribution_summary: string; imported_at: Date }\n938:   | undefined;\n939: \n940: // THE VOLUNTEER'S PRE-CONFIRMATION REFUSAL GETS THE SAME TEETH AS (c)'s, per the same ruling: a\n941: // claim that the volunteer \"repeats (a)-(d)\" is only true if it is measured the same way.\n942: const volunteerRefusal = unconfirmedSignInRefusal(volunteerTrip.signInBefore);\n943: \n944: record(\n945:   'e',\n946:   'both email-capable types flow end to end under the flipped config: the NGO completes after confirmation, and the volunteer confirms, links GitHub and completes with its profile populated',\n947:   // The NGO half.\n948:   ngoCompletion.status === 200 &&\n949:     ngoRows.length === 1 &&\n950:     ngoRows[0].account_type === 'ngo' &&\n951:     ngoRows[0].name === 'Riverside Shelter' &&\n952:     ngoRows[0].role === 'admin' &&\n953:     // The volunteer half: its OWN round trip repeated (a)-(d), then completion.\n954:     volunteerTrip.signupCarriedSession === false &&\n955:     volunteerTrip.confirmedBefore === null &&\n956:     // THE REFUSAL IS THE CONFIRMATION ONE, not merely a 4xx — the same predicate (c) uses.\n957:     volunteerRefusal.ok &&\n958:     // AND THE LINK CAME FROM THE CATCHER. Without this, the admin-minted fallback could carry the\n959:     // volunteer half while the claim \"repeats (b)\" quietly narrowed to something smaller. The NGO\n960:     // half already cannot do that, because (b) fails with a NARROWED note on the fallback.\n961:     volunteerTrip.linkSource === 'emailed' &&\n962:     volunteerTrip.confirmedAfter !== null &&\n963:     volunteerTrip.shippedVerdict === true &&\n964:     volunteerCompletion.status === 200 &&\n965:     volunteerAccountRows.length === 1 &&\n966:     volunteerAccountRows[0].account_type === 'volunteer' &&\n967:     volunteerProfileRows.length === 1 &&\n968:     volunteerProfile?.github_handle === VOLUNTEER_HANDLE &&\n969:     JSON.stringify(volunteerProfile?.top_languages) === JSON.stringify(expectedStats.topLanguages) &&\n970:     Number(volunteerProfile?.repository_count) === expectedStats.repositoryCount &&\n971:     volunteerProfile?.contribution_summary === expectedStats.contributionSummary,\n972:   `NGO completion HTTP ${ngoCompletion.status} ${redact(ngoCompletion.body)} · rows=${JSON.stringify(ngoRows)}\\n        ` +\n973:     `volunteer round trip: signup carried a session=${volunteerTrip.signupCarriedSession}` +\n974:     ` · confirmed before=${volunteerTrip.confirmedBefore ?? 'NULL'} after=${volunteerTrip.confirmedAfter ?? 'NULL'}` +\n975:     ` · sign-in before confirmation ${volunteerRefusal.discriminator}, redacted body ${redact(volunteerTrip.signInBefore.body)}` +\n976:     ` · link source ${volunteerTrip.linkSource} (${volunteerTrip.linkEvidence})` +\n977:     ` · its own tampered-token probe: attempted=${volunteerTrip.tampered.attempted}` +\n978:     ` email_confirmed_at afterwards=${volunteerTrip.tampered.confirmedAfter ?? 'NULL'}` +\n979:     ` · shipped emailVerifiedFromUser says ${volunteerTrip.shippedVerdict}\\n        ` +\n980:     `volunteer completion HTTP ${volunteerCompletion.status} ${redact(volunteerCompletion.body)}` +\n981:     ` · account=${JSON.stringify(volunteerAccountRows)} · stored profile=${JSON.stringify(volunteerProfileRows)}` +\n982:     ` · the shipped stub's judgement for \"${VOLUNTEER_HANDLE}\"=${JSON.stringify(expectedStats)}`,\n983: );\n984: \n985: /* =============================================================================================\n986:  * (f) THE MAILER RATE LIMIT — reported either way, so a starved run can never look like a clean\n987:  *     one.\n988:  *\n989:  * `[auth.rate_limit] email_sent` caps auth emails per hour, and this run sends exactly TWO\n\n(Output capped at 50 KB. Showing lines 1-989. Use offset=990 to continue.)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"/**\n * THE LOCAL PROOF for AI4DEV-59 — the only evidence in this item about the real Supabase Auth\n * under `enable_confirmations = true`, the real confirmation email, and the real\n * `/auth/v1/user` serialisation the shipped extractor has to read.\n *\n *   bun loop/items/AI4DEV-59/proof-local.ts\n *\n * Preconditions, and the script refuses rather than guesses if any is missing:\n *   1. `bun run db:start` — the stack is up, WITH this item's `supabase/config.toml`. The flip to\n *      `enable_confirmations = true` is read at Auth start-up, so a stack started before the flip\n *      is running the old setting and every check below would measure the wrong system.\n *   2. `bun run db:reset` — run it immediately before this script. The checks assert row counts.\n *   3. `bunx supabase functions serve` — running in another terminal, FROM THIS WORKTREE, WITHOUT\n *      --no-verify-jwt. The directory matters and is not pedantry: the edge runtime container\n *      mounts whichever project directory launched it, and this machine was once found serving a\n *      worktree that had since been deleted. A stale mount answers preflights perfectly while\n *      running somebody else's code. The script probes the function before using it.\n *\n * IT LIVES IN THE ITEM RECORD AND NOT IN `tests/`, DELIBERATELY, for the reason the predecessor's\n * copy of this file gives: it is evidence-gathering. It runs once, on one machine, by hand, and","truncated":true,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"C:\\Users\\nirdr\\Downloads\\ai4good\\.claude\\worktrees\\agent-aa95110cbeab31aa7\\loop\\items\\AI4DEV-59\\proof-local.ts","text":"/**\n * THE LOCAL PROOF for AI4DEV-59 — the only evidence in this item about the real Supabase Auth\n * under `enable_confirmations = true`, the real confirmation email, and the real\n * `/auth/v1/user` serialisation the shipped extractor has to read.\n *\n *   bun loop/items/AI4DEV-59/proof-local.ts\n *\n * Preconditions, and the script refuses rather than guesses if any is missing:\n *   1. `bun run db:start` — the stack is up, WITH this item's `supabase/config.toml`. The flip to\n *      `enable_confirmations = true` is read at Auth start-up, so a stack started before the flip\n *      is running the old setting and every check below would measure the wrong system.\n *   2. `bun run db:reset` — run it immediately before this script. The checks assert row counts.\n *   3. `bunx supabase functions serve` — running in another terminal, FROM THIS WORKTREE, WITHOUT\n *      --no-verify-jwt. The directory matters and is not pedantry: the edge runtime container\n *      mounts whichever project directory launched it, and this machine was once found serving a\n *      worktree that had since been deleted. A stale mount answers preflights perfectly while\n *      running somebody else's code. The script probes the function before using it.\n *\n * IT LIVES IN THE ITEM RECORD AND NOT IN `tests/`, DELIBERATELY, for the reason the predecessor's\n * copy of this file gives: it is evidence-gathering. It runs once, on one machine, by hand, and\n * its transcript is the artifact. Under `tests/` it would look like something that guards the\n * tree, and it guards nothing — nothing runs it again and CI has no database.\n *\n * WHAT THE LOOP-TIER SUITE ALREADY PROVES, so this does not duplicate it: that the shipped\n * decisions in `_shared/verification.ts` behave as AT-001.09 and AT-001.10 require, including the\n * fail-closed answers, which `tests/at/harness/shipped-verification.selftest.ts` drives directly.\n * What NOTHING BUT THIS SCRIPT touches: that GoTrue issues no session at signup with confirmations\n * on, that it refuses sign-in until the address is confirmed, that a confirmation email really\n * arrives and its link really flips `email_confirmed_at`, and that the shipped extractor answers\n * true against GoTrue's REAL serialisation rather than against the fixture's prediction of it.\n *\n * ============================================================================================\n * NO CHECK HERE EXERCISES `discoveryMessageAllowed`, AND THAT IS NOT AN OVERSIGHT.\n * ============================================================================================\n *\n * There is no Discovery route on this stack or on any stack — it is REQ-002/004's, and this leaf\n * ships the decision that route must call rather than the route. So there is nothing live to send\n * a Discovery message to. The gate's oracle is the loop-tier body for AT-001.10 plus the direct\n * shape selftest, and this transcript says so instead of leaving a reader to assume a live check\n * happened. What this script DOES add on that subject is check (c): on the live stack an\n * unconfirmed user cannot authenticate at all, so the auth layer blocks upstream of the gate.\n *\n * NO KEY IS WRITTEN INTO THIS FILE AND NONE IS PRINTED BY IT. Everything is read from\n * `bunx supabase status -o json` at run time. Redaction is DESIGNED IN rather than applied\n * afterwards, and it covers THREE surfaces, because gate 2 found the first version covering only\n * one:\n *\n *   1. BODIES. `redact()` below walks every object body before it reaches the transcript and\n *      replaces the value under any credential-shaped key name.\n *   2. STRINGS, including a body that is not JSON at all. `redact()` also scrubs JWT-shaped\n *      substrings and `name=value` pairs whose name is credential-shaped, so a raw string answer\n *      cannot carry a token through verbatim.\n *   3. THE REDIRECT LOCATION. `redactedLocation()` cuts the URL fragment — that fragment is where\n *      GoTrue puts the access token it mints when a link is followed — AND replaces EVERY\n *      query-parameter VALUE, keeping the parameter names. A `?token=` or `?code=` redirect can\n *      therefore not land in a file that gets pushed.\n *\n * GitHub push protection has already refused one push in this project for a transcript that\n * captured a status command verbatim. The transcript is ALSO inspected for credential residue by\n * hand before it is committed; that inspection is recorded in the item's report.\n */\n\nimport { readFileSync } from 'node:fs';\nimport { SQL } from 'bun';\n// THE SHIPPED EXTRACTOR, IMPORTED RATHER THAN MIRRORED — the whole point of check (d). The\n// question this script exists to settle is whether the function that ships answers correctly\n// about a REAL GoTrue response, so re-implementing the read here would settle nothing.\nimport { emailVerifiedFromUser } from '../../../supabase/functions/_shared/verification.ts';\n// The stub import fixture, for the volunteer completion read-back in (e) — the same reason the\n// predecessor imports it: comparing the stored profile against the shipped judgement shows the\n// values travelled from the edge function into the transaction unchanged, where \"the row is\n// non-empty\" would pass over plausible-looking rubbish.\nimport { stubGithubStatsFor } from '../../../supabase/functions/_shared/github.ts';\n\n/* ------------------------------------------------------------------------ reporting machinery */\n\ntype Outcome = 'pass' | 'fail' | 'skip';\ntype Check = { id: string; title: string; outcome: Outcome; note: string };\n\nconst results: Check[] = [];\n\nfunction record(id: string, title: string, passed: boolean, note: string): void {\n  results.push({ id, title, outcome: passed ? 'pass' : 'fail', note });\n  console.log(`${passed ? 'PASS' : 'FAIL'}  (${id}) ${title}\\n        ${note}`);\n}\n\n/**\n * A check that could not be attempted. Never a pass and never a failure — a skip says the evidence\n * is MISSING, which is a different statement from the claim being false, and both differ from the\n * claim being proved.\n */\nfunction skip(id: string, title: string, why: string): void {\n  results.push({ id, title, outcome: 'skip', note: `SKIPPED — ${why}` });\n  console.log(`SKIP  (${id}) ${title}\\n        ${why}`);\n}\n\nfunction fail(message: string): never {\n  console.error(`\\nPRECONDITION FAILED: ${message}`);\n  process.exit(2);\n}\n\n/**\n * EVERY BODY GOES THROUGH THIS BEFORE IT REACHES THE TRANSCRIPT.\n *\n * The fields that carry secrets are known and few — GoTrue answers with `access_token`,\n * `refresh_token`, `provider_token` and friends — but the rule here is by NAME PATTERN rather than\n * by a list, because a list is a thing to forget to extend. A value under any key whose name looks\n * like a credential is replaced; the KEY still appears, so the transcript still shows that the\n * field was present, which is usually the fact a check needs.\n *\n * It returns a STRING, not an object, so there is no path by which an unredacted value reaches the\n * output through some later `JSON.stringify`.\n */\nconst SENSITIVE = /token|secret|password|apikey|api_key|jwt|nonce|otp|code$/i;\n\n/**\n * A STRING SCRUBBED OF CREDENTIALS, for the surface `redact()`'s key walk cannot see.\n *\n * A key walk protects `{\"access_token\": \"...\"}`. It protects nothing inside a value — and GoTrue\n * answers with raw strings on some paths, while any body at all can carry a link with a token in\n * its query. So two shapes are replaced here:\n *\n *   - A JWT. Three base64url runs separated by dots, beginning `eyJ`, which is what a JSON header\n *     always encodes to. Every access token, refresh token and service key in this stack is one.\n *   - A `name=value` pair whose NAME is credential-shaped by the same pattern the key walk uses.\n *     The name survives, so the transcript still shows which parameter was present.\n */\nfunction scrubString(text: string): string {\n  return text\n    .replace(/eyJ[A-Za-z0-9_-]+\\.[A-Za-z0-9_-]+\\.[A-Za-z0-9_-]+/g, '<redacted-jwt>')\n    .replace(/([A-Za-z0-9_.-]+)=([^&\\s\"'<>]+)/g, (whole, name: string) =>\n      SENSITIVE.test(name) ? `${name}=<redacted>` : whole,\n    );\n}\n\nfunction redact(value: unknown): string {\n  const walk = (node: unknown): unknown => {\n    if (Array.isArray(node)) return node.map(walk);\n    if (node !== null && typeof node === 'object') {\n      const out: Record<string, unknown> = {};\n      for (const [key, inner] of Object.entries(node as Record<string, unknown>)) {\n        out[key] = SENSITIVE.test(key) ? '<redacted>' : walk(inner);\n      }\n      return out;\n    }\n    // A STRING VALUE IS SCRUBBED TOO. This is the half gate 2 found missing: a body that is not\n    // JSON reaches here as one long string, and a key walk never looks inside it.\n    if (typeof node === 'string') return scrubString(node);\n    return node;\n  };\n  try {\n    return JSON.stringify(walk(value));\n  } catch {\n    return '<unserialisable>';\n  }\n}\n\n/**\n * A URL SAFE TO PRINT: no fragment, and no query-parameter VALUES.\n *\n * The fragment is where GoTrue puts the minted access token. The QUERY is where it puts the\n * token hash, the `code`, and the error description — and printing those unchanged was the defect\n * two blind reviewers found independently. Parameter NAMES are kept, because which parameters a\n * redirect carried is usually the fact a check needs, and a name is not a credential.\n */\nfunction redactedLocation(url: string): string {\n  if (url === '') return '';\n  const hash = url.indexOf('#');\n  const fragmentNote = hash < 0 ? '' : '#<redacted-fragment>';\n  const withoutHash = hash < 0 ? url : url.slice(0, hash);\n\n  const question = withoutHash.indexOf('?');\n  if (question < 0) return `${withoutHash}${fragmentNote}`;\n\n  const query = withoutHash\n    .slice(question + 1)\n    .split('&')\n    .map((pair) => {\n      const equals = pair.indexOf('=');\n      // A bare flag has no value to redact.\n      return equals < 0 ? pair : `${pair.slice(0, equals)}=<redacted>`;\n    })\n    .join('&');\n  return `${withoutHash.slice(0, question)}?${query}${fragmentNote}`;\n}\n\n/* --------------------------------------------------------------------------- the environment */\n\nconst status = await (async () => {\n  const proc = Bun.spawnSync(['bunx', 'supabase', 'status', '-o', 'json'], { stdout: 'pipe', stderr: 'pipe' });\n  const text = new TextDecoder().decode(proc.stdout);\n  const start = text.indexOf('{');\n  // The raw text is NOT included in this message, deliberately: it is the whole status payload and\n  // it carries key material. A failure here says what to do, not what it read.\n  if (start < 0) fail('could not read `supabase status -o json` — is the stack up? (bun run db:start)');\n  return JSON.parse(text.slice(start)) as Record<string, string>;\n})();\n\nconst API_URL = status.API_URL ?? fail('supabase status reported no API_URL');\nconst DB_URL = status.DB_URL ?? fail('supabase status reported no DB_URL');\n/** The browser's key. Everything a signed-out or signed-in visitor could do uses this and no other. */\nconst PUBLIC_KEY = status.ANON_KEY ?? status.PUBLISHABLE_KEY ?? fail('supabase status reported no client key');\n/** The server's key. Used ONLY where the check is about an authority the public never holds. */\nconst SECRET_KEY = status.SERVICE_ROLE_KEY ?? status.SECRET_KEY ?? fail('supabase status reported no service key');\n/**\n * The local mail catcher. Its port is 54324 in `supabase/config.toml`, and its API SHAPE is\n * measured below rather than assumed — see `discoverMailCatcher`.\n */\nconst MAIL_URL = status.MAILPIT_URL ?? status.INBUCKET_URL ?? 'http://127.0.0.1:54324';\n\nconst sql = new SQL(DB_URL);\n\nconst TEXT_VERSION = 'tos-2026-01+promise-2026-01';\nconst PASSWORD = 'correct horse battery staple';\nconst CLIENT_IP = '203.0.113.7';\nconst VOLUNTEER_HANDLE = 'octoverifier';\n/** Unique per run, so a second run against a database that was not reset still reads honestly. */\nconst RUN = Date.now().toString(36);\nconst address = (local: string) => `${local}+${RUN}@example.test`;\n\n/**\n * The configured per-hour cap on auth emails, read from the file rather than remembered.\n *\n * This run sends TWO confirmation emails — one per email-capable account type — and the checked-in\n * value is 2, so the file's cap sits EXACTLY on what this run sends — which is why check (f)\n * exists and why the plan pre-authorized raising it.\n *\n * MEASURED ON THE FIRST RUN: the CLI did NOT push this file value into the auth container — the\n * container carried GOTRUE_RATE_LIMIT_EMAIL_SENT=360000, recorded in stack-up.txt beside the\n * reading — so the file's cap did not bind that run. Check (f) still reports the file's value AND\n * whether any mailer refusal appeared, which together are the honest pair: the transcript shows\n * what the file claims and what the mailer actually did.\n */\nconst CONFIGURED_EMAIL_RATE_LIMIT = (() => {\n  try {\n    const toml = readFileSync(new URL('../../../supabase/config.toml', import.meta.url), 'utf8');\n    return /^\\s*email_sent\\s*=\\s*(\\d+)/m.exec(toml)?.[1] ?? '<not found>';\n  } catch {\n    return '<unreadable>';\n  }\n})();\n\n/**\n * Anything that looks like the mailer refusing to send. Check (f) reports on this, so a starved\n * run can never look like a clean one.\n */\nconst rateLimitEvidence: string[] = [];\n\nfunction noteIfRateLimited(where: string, httpStatus: number, body: unknown): void {\n  const text = typeof body === 'string' ? body : JSON.stringify(body ?? null);\n  if (httpStatus === 429 || /rate.?limit|over_email_send/i.test(text)) {\n    rateLimitEvidence.push(`${where}: HTTP ${httpStatus} ${redact(body)}`);\n  }\n}\n\n/* --------------------------------------------------------------------------------- Auth calls */\n\ntype Wire = { status: number; body: unknown };\n\nasync function authPost(path: string, body: unknown, key = PUBLIC_KEY): Promise<Wire> {\n  const response = await fetch(`${API_URL}${path}`, {\n    method: 'POST',\n    headers: { 'content-type': 'application/json', apikey: key, Authorization: `Bearer ${key}` },\n    body: JSON.stringify(body),\n  });\n  const text = await response.text();\n  let parsed: unknown = text;\n  try {\n    parsed = JSON.parse(text);\n  } catch {\n    // A non-JSON answer is itself evidence; it is kept as the raw string.\n  }\n  return { status: response.status, body: parsed };\n}\n\n/**\n * Register an email/password user. WITH CONFIRMATIONS ON THIS MUST RETURN NO SESSION, which is\n * check (a)'s first half, so the caller inspects the wire response rather than being handed a\n * tidy session object.\n *\n * THE USER ID IS TAKEN FROM THE RESPONSE WHEN IT IS THERE, AND FROM THE DATABASE WHEN IT IS NOT.\n * GoTrue's confirmations-on signup response has varied across versions — some return the full user\n * record, some return an obfuscated one to avoid leaking whether an address already exists. Which\n * one this stack does is recorded rather than assumed, and neither answer is a failure: the id is\n * an operator read either way.\n */\nasync function signUp(email: string): Promise<{ wire: Wire; userId: string; idFrom: 'response' | 'database' }> {\n  const wire = await authPost('/auth/v1/signup', { email, password: PASSWORD });\n  noteIfRateLimited(`signup ${email}`, wire.status, wire.body);\n  if (wire.status >= 400) fail(`sign-up for ${email} failed (HTTP ${wire.status}): ${redact(wire.body)}`);\n\n  const body = wire.body as { id?: string; user?: { id?: string } } | null;\n  const fromResponse = body?.user?.id ?? body?.id;\n  if (typeof fromResponse === 'string' && fromResponse !== '') {\n    return { wire, userId: fromResponse, idFrom: 'response' };\n  }\n  const rows = await sql`select id from auth.users where email = ${email}`;\n  if (rows.length !== 1) fail(`sign-up for ${email} left ${rows.length} rows in auth.users — expected exactly 1`);\n  return { wire, userId: String(rows[0].id), idFrom: 'database' };\n}\n\n/** Sign in with a password. Returns the wire answer AND the token, which is never printed. */\nasync function signIn(email: string): Promise<{ wire: Wire; accessToken: string | null }> {\n  const wire = await authPost('/auth/v1/token?grant_type=password', { email, password: PASSWORD });\n  const token = (wire.body as { access_token?: string } | null)?.access_token;\n  return { wire, accessToken: typeof token === 'string' && token !== '' ? token : null };\n}\n\n/**\n * GoTrue's error code for \"this address is not confirmed yet\".\n *\n * It is the DISCRIMINATOR check (c) needs, and it is pinned as a constant so the transcript states\n * which value was expected. If the live stack answers a different code, the check FAILS and the\n * verbatim capture beside it shows the real one — measurement, never assumption.\n */\nconst CONFIRMATION_ERROR_CODE = 'email_not_confirmed';\n\n/**\n * IS THIS THE CONFIRMATION REFUSAL, or merely A refusal — the distinction gate 2 found missing.\n *\n * `status >= 400` alone passes on a 429 from the mailer's rate limiter and on a 500 from a broken\n * stack, and either would record \"confirmation is enforced\" while confirmation enforced nothing.\n * So a pass needs BOTH halves:\n *\n *   - a 4xx that is NOT 429 — a client refusal, not a throttle and not a server fault;\n *   - a body that NAMES the unconfirmed state — the pinned error code, or `confirm` in the\n *     message, which is the weaker initial predicate the ruling allows.\n *\n * IT READS THE IN-MEMORY BODY, NOT THE PRINTED ONE, and that is load-bearing: the transcript\n * redactor's key pattern ends with `code$`, so `error_code` is blanked in everything it prints.\n * Judging the printed text would judge `<redacted>`. The discriminator is surfaced separately\n * below through a safe extraction — an error code is an enum value, not a credential.\n */\nfunction unconfirmedSignInRefusal(wire: Wire): { ok: boolean; discriminator: string } {\n  const body =\n    wire.body !== null && typeof wire.body === 'object'\n      ? (wire.body as Record<string, unknown>)\n      : {};\n  const errorCode = typeof body.error_code === 'string' ? body.error_code : '';\n  const message = [body.msg, body.message, body.error_description, body.error, wire.body]\n    .filter((part): part is string => typeof part === 'string')\n    .join(' | ');\n\n  const statusIsClientRefusal = wire.status >= 400 && wire.status < 500 && wire.status !== 429;\n  const bodyNamesTheState = errorCode === CONFIRMATION_ERROR_CODE || /confirm/i.test(message);\n\n  return {\n    ok: statusIsClientRefusal && bodyNamesTheState,\n    discriminator:\n      `HTTP ${wire.status} · error_code=${errorCode === '' ? '<absent>' : errorCode}` +\n      ` (expected ${CONFIRMATION_ERROR_CODE}) · message=${JSON.stringify(scrubString(message) || '<absent>')}`,\n  };\n}\n\n/**\n * THE SAME LINK WITH A TOKEN GOTRUE NEVER ISSUED — check (b2)'s instrument.\n *\n * The mirror under test says a link that was never issued confirms nothing, and following a\n * MUTATED token is how that is measured without touching any semantics REQ-001 does not state.\n * The token this returns was never minted, so following it says nothing about expiry, about single\n * use, or about resend — retired AT-001.11 stays untouched. Every other part of the link, the\n * `type` and the redirect included, is left exactly as GoTrue wrote it, so the only difference\n * between this request and the real one is the token itself.\n *\n * The mutation shifts digits and letters inside their own alphabet, which keeps the token the same\n * length and shape. A token that changed shape could be refused for being malformed rather than\n * for being unissued, and the check would then measure input validation instead of the mirror.\n */\nfunction tamperVerificationToken(link: string): { tampered: string; parameter: string } | null {\n  let url: URL;\n  try {\n    url = new URL(link);\n  } catch {\n    return null;\n  }\n  const parameter = ['token', 'token_hash', 'confirmation_token'].find((name) => {\n    const value = url.searchParams.get(name);\n    return typeof value === 'string' && value !== '';\n  });\n  if (parameter === undefined) return null;\n\n  const original = url.searchParams.get(parameter) as string;\n  const mutated = original.replace(/[0-9a-zA-Z]/g, (character) => {\n    if (character >= '0' && character <= '9') return String((Number(character) + 5) % 10);\n    const base = character >= 'a' ? 'a'.charCodeAt(0) : 'A'.charCodeAt(0);\n    return String.fromCharCode(base + ((character.charCodeAt(0) - base + 13) % 26));\n  });\n  if (mutated === original) return null;\n\n  url.searchParams.set(parameter, mutated);\n  return { tampered: url.toString(), parameter };\n}\n\n/** `auth.users.email_confirmed_at` read as the operator, on port 54322 — not through any API. */\nasync function confirmedAt(userId: string): Promise<string | null> {\n  const rows = await sql`select email_confirmed_at from auth.users where id = ${userId}::uuid`;\n  const value = rows[0]?.email_confirmed_at ?? null;\n  return value === null ? null : String(value instanceof Date ? value.toISOString() : value);\n}\n\n/**\n * THE POST-LINK AUTH STATE FOR GITHUB, FABRICATED BY OPERATOR AUTHORITY — carried over from the\n * predecessor item unchanged, including the correction that cost it real time.\n *\n * Linking a GitHub identity is an OAuth consent round trip: a person at github.com and a redirect\n * no script can drive. What linking LEAVES BEHIND is a row in `auth.identities`, and that row is\n * what the shipped code reads. So the row is written directly, as the operator. IT IS\n * FLIP-INDEPENDENT — a database write, not an auth flow — which is why (e) can reuse it under the\n * new setting without re-proving it.\n *\n * `created_at`, `updated_at` and `last_sign_in_at` are NULLABLE in the table, so a row without them\n * inserts happily; but GoTrue reads identities into a Go struct with non-pointer timestamps, so\n * `GET /auth/v1/user` then answers 500 for that user, and the whole thing looks exactly like an\n * expired token rather than like a malformed row three layers away. So they are filled here.\n */\nasync function fabricateGithubIdentity(userId: string, handle: string): Promise<void> {\n  await sql`\n    insert into auth.identities (id, user_id, provider, provider_id, identity_data,\n                                 created_at, updated_at, last_sign_in_at)\n    values (\n      gen_random_uuid(),\n      ${userId}::uuid,\n      'github',\n      ${`gh-${handle}-${RUN}`},\n      -- The casts are explicit because jsonb_build_object takes \"any\", so PostgreSQL has nothing to\n      -- infer a parameter's type from and refuses the statement outright rather than guessing.\n      jsonb_build_object('sub', ${userId}::text, 'user_name', ${handle}::text, 'provider_id', ${`gh-${handle}-${RUN}`}::text),\n      now(), now(), now()\n    )`;\n}\n\n/* ------------------------------------------------------------------------ the edge function */\n\nasync function callFunction(name: string, accessToken: string, body: unknown): Promise<Wire> {\n  const response = await fetch(`${API_URL}/functions/v1/${name}`, {\n    method: 'POST',\n    headers: {\n      'content-type': 'application/json',\n      apikey: PUBLIC_KEY,\n      Authorization: `Bearer ${accessToken}`,\n      'x-forwarded-for': CLIENT_IP,\n    },\n    body: JSON.stringify(body),\n  });\n  const text = await response.text();\n  let parsed: unknown = text;\n  try {\n    parsed = JSON.parse(text);\n  } catch {\n    /* kept raw */\n  }\n  return { status: response.status, body: parsed };\n}\n\n// The stale-mount hazard the header names: probe the function before any check depends on it. An\n// unauthenticated POST must be REFUSED by something, and a connection error is a different failure\n// from a refusal. Only the second means the function is being served.\n//\n// THE PROBE GUARDS REACHABILITY, NOT REVISION, AND (e)'s EVIDENCE DOES NOT NEED IT TO.\n// A reviewer read this as a hole: a stale mount serving an older worktree answers 401, so any\n// non-404 passes and the probe cannot tell which REVISION of `complete-signup` replied. The fact is\n// right; the consequence is not, and the reason was verified in the tree before this comment was\n// written. This branch changes NOTHING under `supabase/functions/` except the NEW\n// `_shared/verification.ts` (`git diff main...HEAD --stat -- supabase/functions/`: one file, 167\n// insertions), and `complete-signup/index.ts` imports only `_shared/accounts.ts`,\n// `_shared/github.ts` and `_shared/edge.ts` — never `verification.ts`. So every revision of\n// `complete-signup` this branch could be compared against is byte for byte the same code, and the\n// mount's revision is not load-bearing for anything (e) claims.\n// The operator side of the binding is recorded rather than inferred:\n// `loop/items/AI4DEV-59/stack-up.txt` carries the exact `bunx supabase functions serve` command and\n// the directory it ran in.\nawait (async () => {\n  try {\n    const probe = await fetch(`${API_URL}/functions/v1/complete-signup`, {\n      method: 'POST',\n      headers: { 'content-type': 'application/json', apikey: PUBLIC_KEY },\n      body: '{}',\n    });\n    if (probe.status === 404) {\n      fail('the edge runtime answered 404 for complete-signup — `bunx supabase functions serve` is not serving THIS worktree');\n    }\n  } catch (error) {\n    fail(\n      'could not reach the edge runtime at all — run `bunx supabase functions serve` from THIS worktree ' +\n        `(${error instanceof Error ? error.message : String(error)})`,\n    );\n  }\n})();\n\n/* ------------------------------------------------------------------- the mail catcher, MEASURED */\n\ntype CatcherKind = 'mailpit' | 'inbucket' | 'unknown';\n\n/**\n * WHICH MAIL CATCHER IS BEHIND PORT 54324, established by asking it rather than by remembering.\n *\n * The Supabase CLI has shipped Inbucket and, more recently, Mailpit, and their APIs are not the\n * same shape. The plan's risk 2 is exactly this. So both are probed, BOTH ANSWERS GO IN THE\n * TRANSCRIPT, and the extraction below branches on what actually replied. An assumption here would\n * fail as \"no confirmation email arrived\", which is indistinguishable from the mailer being\n * rate-limited — the single most misleading way this script could go wrong.\n */\nasync function discoverMailCatcher(): Promise<{ kind: CatcherKind; evidence: string }> {\n  const probe = async (path: string): Promise<string> => {\n    try {\n      const response = await fetch(`${MAIL_URL}${path}`);\n      const text = (await response.text()).slice(0, 200);\n      return `GET ${path} -> HTTP ${response.status} ${JSON.stringify(text)}`;\n    } catch (error) {\n      return `GET ${path} -> unreachable (${error instanceof Error ? error.message : String(error)})`;\n    }\n  };\n\n  const mailpit = await probe('/api/v1/info');\n  const inbucket = await probe('/api/v1/mailbox/probe');\n  const evidence = `catcher at ${MAIL_URL} · ${mailpit} · ${inbucket}`;\n\n  if (/HTTP 200/.test(mailpit)) return { kind: 'mailpit', evidence };\n  if (/HTTP 200/.test(inbucket)) return { kind: 'inbucket', evidence };\n  return { kind: 'unknown', evidence };\n}\n\nconst catcher = await discoverMailCatcher();\nconsole.log(`\\nmail catcher shape, measured: ${catcher.kind}\\n        ${catcher.evidence}\\n`);\n\n/**\n * Every URL in the message that looks like GoTrue's verify endpoint.\n *\n * `&amp;` IS DECODED FIRST, and that is not cosmetic: the confirmation email's HTML part escapes\n * the ampersands, so a link lifted straight out of it carries `&amp;type=signup` and GoTrue reads\n * the parameter name as `amp;type`. The failure looks like a rejected link rather than like a\n * mangled one.\n */\nfunction verifyLinksIn(text: string): string[] {\n  const decoded = text.replace(/&amp;/g, '&');\n  return decoded.match(/https?:\\/\\/[^\\s\"'<>)\\]]+\\/auth\\/v1\\/verify[^\\s\"'<>)\\]]*/g) ?? [];\n}\n\nasync function emailedVerificationLink(email: string): Promise<{ link: string | null; evidence: string }> {\n  const local = email.split('@')[0];\n  try {\n    if (catcher.kind === 'mailpit') {\n      const list = await fetch(`${MAIL_URL}/api/v1/search?query=${encodeURIComponent(`to:${email}`)}&limit=20`);\n      const listed = (await list.json()) as { messages?: Array<{ ID?: string }> };\n      const id = listed.messages?.[0]?.ID;\n      if (!id) return { link: null, evidence: `mailpit search for ${email} returned no message` };\n      const message = await fetch(`${MAIL_URL}/api/v1/message/${id}`);\n      const parsed = (await message.json()) as { Text?: string; HTML?: string };\n      const links = verifyLinksIn(`${parsed.Text ?? ''}\\n${parsed.HTML ?? ''}`);\n      return {\n        link: links[0] ?? null,\n        evidence: `mailpit held ${listed.messages?.length ?? 0} message(s) for ${email}; ${links.length} verify link(s) found`,\n      };\n    }\n    if (catcher.kind === 'inbucket') {\n      const list = await fetch(`${MAIL_URL}/api/v1/mailbox/${encodeURIComponent(local)}`);\n      const listed = (await list.json()) as Array<{ id?: string }>;\n      const id = listed[0]?.id;\n      if (!id) return { link: null, evidence: `inbucket mailbox ${local} was empty` };\n      const message = await fetch(`${MAIL_URL}/api/v1/mailbox/${encodeURIComponent(local)}/${id}`);\n      const parsed = (await message.json()) as { body?: { text?: string; html?: string } };\n      const links = verifyLinksIn(`${parsed.body?.text ?? ''}\\n${parsed.body?.html ?? ''}`);\n      return {\n        link: links[0] ?? null,\n        evidence: `inbucket mailbox ${local} held ${listed.length} message(s); ${links.length} verify link(s) found`,\n      };\n    }\n    return { link: null, evidence: `the catcher at ${MAIL_URL} matched no known API shape` };\n  } catch (error) {\n    return { link: null, evidence: `reading the catcher failed: ${error instanceof Error ? error.message : String(error)}` };\n  }\n}\n\n/**\n * THE FALLBACK THE PLAN'S RISK 2 PRE-AUTHORIZES, and it NARROWS the claim rather than rescuing it.\n *\n * GoTrue's admin endpoint mints a verification link with operator authority. If the catcher cannot\n * yield the emailed link, this is what gets followed — and the transcript then says, in the check's\n * own note, that the link followed was NOT the emailed one. That is a smaller claim, stated as a\n * smaller claim. It is never used when the catcher works.\n */\nasync function adminGeneratedLink(email: string): Promise<{ link: string | null; evidence: string }> {\n  const wire = await authPost('/auth/v1/admin/generate_link', { type: 'signup', email, password: PASSWORD }, SECRET_KEY);\n  const link = (wire.body as { action_link?: string } | null)?.action_link ?? null;\n  return {\n    link: typeof link === 'string' && link !== '' ? link : null,\n    evidence: `admin generate_link -> HTTP ${wire.status} (action_link ${link ? 'present' : 'absent'})`,\n  };\n}\n\n/* ============================================================================================\n * THE VERIFICATION ROUND TRIP — (a) through (d), as one function so the SECOND address in (e)\n * repeats exactly the same measurement rather than an abbreviated version of it.\n * ============================================================================================ */\n\n/**\n * What following a tampered link did — or why it could not be followed at all.\n *\n * `attempted: false` is never a pass. Check (b2) SKIPS on it, because a check that could not run\n * is a missing measurement rather than a satisfied one.\n */\ntype TamperedFollow = {\n  attempted: boolean;\n  why: string;\n  parameter: string;\n  status: number;\n  location: string;\n  confirmedAfter: string | null;\n};\n\ntype RoundTrip = {\n  email: string;\n  userId: string;\n  idFrom: 'response' | 'database';\n  signupWire: Wire;\n  /** the whole point of (a): with confirmations on there must be NO token in the signup answer */\n  signupCarriedSession: boolean;\n  confirmedBefore: string | null;\n  linkSource: 'emailed' | 'admin-generated' | 'none';\n  linkEvidence: string;\n  signInBefore: Wire;\n  /** check (b2): the never-issued-token negative, measured BEFORE the real link is followed */\n  tampered: TamperedFollow;\n  followStatus: number;\n  followLocation: string;\n  confirmedAfter: string | null;\n  signInAfter: Wire;\n  accessToken: string | null;\n  rawUserWire: Wire;\n  shippedVerdict: boolean;\n};\n\nasync function verificationRoundTrip(email: string): Promise<RoundTrip> {\n  const { wire: signupWire, userId, idFrom } = await signUp(email);\n  const signupBody = signupWire.body as { access_token?: unknown; session?: unknown } | null;\n  const signupCarriedSession =\n    typeof signupBody?.access_token === 'string' || (signupBody?.session !== null && signupBody?.session !== undefined);\n\n  const confirmedBefore = await confirmedAt(userId);\n\n  // (c) is measured BEFORE the link is followed, because afterwards the same call succeeds and the\n  // refusal would be unobtainable.\n  const signInBefore = (await signIn(email)).wire;\n\n  const fromCatcher = await emailedVerificationLink(email);\n  let link = fromCatcher.link;\n  let linkSource: RoundTrip['linkSource'] = link ? 'emailed' : 'none';\n  let linkEvidence = fromCatcher.evidence;\n  if (!link) {\n    const fallback = await adminGeneratedLink(email);\n    if (fallback.link) {\n      link = fallback.link;\n      linkSource = 'admin-generated';\n    }\n    linkEvidence = `${fromCatcher.evidence} · FALLBACK: ${fallback.evidence}`;\n  }\n\n  // (b2) THE NEVER-ISSUED TOKEN, FOLLOWED BEFORE THE REAL LINK.\n  //\n  // The order matters and is the whole design of this block: once the real link has confirmed the\n  // address, `email_confirmed_at` is non-null for every later read, and the negative becomes\n  // unmeasurable. So the tampered variant goes first, and the column is read straight afterwards.\n  const tampered: TamperedFollow = {\n    attempted: false,\n    why: '',\n    parameter: '',\n    status: 0,\n    location: '',\n    confirmedAfter: null,\n  };\n  if (!link) {\n    tampered.why = 'no verification link was obtained at all, so there was nothing to tamper with';\n  } else {\n    const mutation = tamperVerificationToken(link);\n    if (mutation === null) {\n      tampered.why = 'the link carries no non-empty token parameter this script knows how to mutate';\n    } else {\n      const followed = await fetch(mutation.tampered, { redirect: 'manual' });\n      tampered.attempted = true;\n      tampered.parameter = mutation.parameter;\n      tampered.status = followed.status;\n      tampered.location = redactedLocation(followed.headers.get('location') ?? '');\n      tampered.confirmedAfter = await confirmedAt(userId);\n    }\n  }\n\n  let followStatus = 0;\n  let followLocation = '';\n  if (link) {\n    const followed = await fetch(link, { redirect: 'manual' });\n    followStatus = followed.status;\n    // THE FRAGMENT IS CUT AND EVERY QUERY VALUE IS REPLACED. GoTrue redirects to the site URL with\n    // the freshly minted access token in the URL fragment, and this string goes into a file that\n    // gets pushed.\n    followLocation = redactedLocation(followed.headers.get('location') ?? '');\n  }\n\n  const confirmedAfter = await confirmedAt(userId);\n  const after = await signIn(email);\n  const rawUserWire: Wire = after.accessToken\n    ? await (async () => {\n        const response = await fetch(`${API_URL}/auth/v1/user`, {\n          headers: { apikey: PUBLIC_KEY, Authorization: `Bearer ${after.accessToken}` },\n        });\n        const text = await response.text();\n        let parsed: unknown = text;\n        try {\n          parsed = JSON.parse(text);\n        } catch {\n          /* kept raw */\n        }\n        return { status: response.status, body: parsed };\n      })()\n    : { status: 0, body: null };\n\n  return {\n    email,\n    userId,\n    idFrom,\n    signupWire,\n    signupCarriedSession,\n    confirmedBefore,\n    linkSource,\n    linkEvidence,\n    signInBefore,\n    tampered,\n    followStatus,\n    followLocation,\n    confirmedAfter,\n    signInAfter: after.wire,\n    accessToken: after.accessToken,\n    rawUserWire,\n    // THE SHIPPED FUNCTION, ON THE REAL BODY. This single line is what binds the extractor to\n    // GoTrue's actual serialisation; everything else in this script is the setup that makes it\n    // meaningful.\n    shippedVerdict: emailVerifiedFromUser(rawUserWire.body),\n  };\n}\n\nconst ngoEmail = address('ngo-verify');\nconst ngoTrip = await verificationRoundTrip(ngoEmail);\n\n/* =============================================================================================\n * (a) SIGNUP UNDER THE FLIPPED CONFIG ISSUES NO SESSION, AND THE ADDRESS IS UNCONFIRMED.\n *\n * The first of the plan's three unverified-runtime-claims, measured rather than expected. Both\n * halves matter: no token on the wire, AND `email_confirmed_at IS NULL` read as the operator on\n * port 54322. The wire answer alone could be a client-library convention; the column is the fact.\n * ============================================================================================= */\n\nrecord(\n  'a',\n  'an email/password signup under enable_confirmations = true returns NO session, and auth.users.email_confirmed_at is NULL',\n  ngoTrip.signupWire.status < 400 && ngoTrip.signupCarriedSession === false && ngoTrip.confirmedBefore === null,\n  `signup HTTP ${ngoTrip.signupWire.status} ${redact(ngoTrip.signupWire.body)}` +\n    ` · carried a session: ${ngoTrip.signupCarriedSession}` +\n    ` · user id read from the ${ngoTrip.idFrom}` +\n    ` · auth.users.email_confirmed_at = ${ngoTrip.confirmedBefore ?? 'NULL'}`,\n);\n\n/* =============================================================================================\n * (b) THE CONFIRMATION EMAIL EXISTS IN THE CATCHER, AND THE LINK COMES OUT OF IT.\n *\n * The catcher's API shape was measured above, not assumed — the evidence line says which shape\n * answered. If the link came from the admin endpoint instead, this check says so IN ITS OWN NOTE\n * and the claim is narrower: an equivalent link was followed, but not the emailed one.\n *\n * AN UNRECOGNISED CATCHER SKIPS RATHER THAN FAILS, and the distinction is the shared invariant\n * about re-measuring a negative: a broken instrument and a true absence produce identical output.\n * \"The mailbox reads empty\" is not evidence that no email was sent when the thing being asked is\n * not the thing that answers. A skip says the evidence is missing, which is what is true.\n * ============================================================================================= */\n\nif (catcher.kind === 'unknown') {\n  skip(\n    'b',\n    'the local mail catcher holds a confirmation email for the address, and the verification link is extracted from it',\n    `the catcher at ${MAIL_URL} matched no known API shape, so this check has no working instrument. ` +\n      `It is NOT evidence that no confirmation email was sent — a broken reader and an empty mailbox look the same. ` +\n      `Probe results: ${catcher.evidence}. Fix the reader, or note which catcher this CLI version ships, then re-run.`,\n  );\n} else {\n  record(\n    'b',\n    'the local mail catcher holds a confirmation email for the address, and the verification link is extracted from it',\n    ngoTrip.linkSource === 'emailed',\n    `catcher shape: ${catcher.kind} · ${ngoTrip.linkEvidence} · link source: ${ngoTrip.linkSource}` +\n      (ngoTrip.linkSource === 'admin-generated'\n        ? ' — NARROWED: the EMAILED link was not what was followed; an operator-minted equivalent was.'\n        : ''),\n  );\n}\n\n/* =============================================================================================\n * (b2) A TOKEN GOTRUE NEVER ISSUED CONFIRMS NOTHING.\n *\n * The fixture's mirror 2 says a link that was never issued has no confirming effect. Two blind\n * reviewers found the same defect: that mirror was labelled BOUND by checks (a)–(d), and none of\n * those checks ever follows an unissued link — the named evidence measured only the positive half.\n * This check is the negative, measured.\n *\n * The instrument is the REAL link with its token mutated inside its own alphabet, followed BEFORE\n * the real one. What it asserts is exactly one thing: `auth.users.email_confirmed_at` is still\n * NULL afterwards. It asserts NOTHING about expiry, single use or resend — the token followed was\n * never minted, so no lifetime and no use count is in play, and retired AT-001.11's semantics stay\n * untouched.\n *\n * THE RESPONSE STATUS IS RECORDED BUT NOT ASSERTED ON. GoTrue may answer a redirect carrying an\n * error, or a 4xx, and which one it picks is its own business. The column is the fact.\n * ============================================================================================= */\n\nif (!ngoTrip.tampered.attempted) {\n  skip(\n    'b2',\n    'a verification link carrying a token GoTrue never issued confirms nothing',\n    `${ngoTrip.tampered.why}. Link source was \"${ngoTrip.linkSource}\" (${ngoTrip.linkEvidence}). ` +\n      'This is NOT evidence that a never-issued token confirms an address — it is evidence that the ' +\n      'negative could not be measured on this run.',\n  );\n} else {\n  record(\n    'b2',\n    'a verification link carrying a token GoTrue never issued confirms nothing',\n    ngoTrip.tampered.confirmedAfter === null,\n    `the \"${ngoTrip.tampered.parameter}\" parameter of the ${ngoTrip.linkSource} link was mutated inside its own alphabet` +\n      ` · following the tampered link answered HTTP ${ngoTrip.tampered.status} -> ${ngoTrip.tampered.location || '<no location header>'}` +\n      ` · auth.users.email_confirmed_at immediately afterwards = ${ngoTrip.tampered.confirmedAfter ?? 'NULL'}` +\n      ' · this says nothing about expiry, single use or resend: the token followed was never issued' +\n      (ngoTrip.linkSource === 'admin-generated'\n        ? ' · NARROWED: the link mutated was the operator-minted one, because the catcher yielded none.'\n        : ''),\n  );\n}\n\n/* =============================================================================================\n * (c) SIGN-IN BEFORE CONFIRMATION IS REFUSED, WITH THE REFUSAL TEXT CAPTURED VERBATIM.\n *\n * The second unverified-runtime-claim. On the live stack this block sits UPSTREAM of the shipped\n * gate: an unconfirmed user cannot authenticate at all, so it never reaches a write path. That is\n * why the gate is not redundant rather than why it is — decision-8 makes verification the write\n * path's own floor, and non-public paths do not pass through this refusal.\n *\n * THE REFUSAL MUST BE THE CONFIRMATION ONE, not any refusal. `status >= 400` was the first\n * version's predicate and a reviewer showed what it accepts: a 429 from the mailer's rate limiter\n * and a 500 from a broken stack both record as PASS while confirmation enforces nothing. See\n * `unconfirmedSignInRefusal` above for the two halves a pass now needs.\n * ============================================================================================= */\n\nconst ngoRefusal = unconfirmedSignInRefusal(ngoTrip.signInBefore);\n\nrecord(\n  'c',\n  'sign-in BEFORE confirmation is refused by Auth itself, with a CONFIRMATION-SPECIFIC refusal (a 4xx that is not 429, naming the unconfirmed state)',\n  ngoRefusal.ok,\n  `sign-in ${ngoRefusal.discriminator}` +\n    ` · redacted body: ${redact(ngoTrip.signInBefore.body)} — captured verbatim, this is the refusal text` +\n    ' · the discriminator above is read from the in-memory body, because the redactor blanks error_code',\n);\n\n/* =============================================================================================\n * (d) USING THE LINK FLIPS THE COLUMN, SIGN-IN THEN SUCCEEDS, AND THE SHIPPED EXTRACTOR AGREES\n *     WITH GOTRUE'S REAL SERIALISATION.\n *\n * The third unverified-runtime-claim and the one this whole script exists for. `emailVerifiedFromUser`\n * is fed the RAW `/auth/v1/user` body — not a shape this script built — and must answer true.\n *\n * ITS FALSE HALF IS NOT EXERCISED HERE, and the transcript says so rather than dressing it up: an\n * unconfirmed user holds no session, so there is no token with which to read `/auth/v1/user` for\n * one. The false half is exercised at the loop tier and in the direct shape selftest.\n * ============================================================================================= */\n\nrecord(\n  'd',\n  'following the verification link flips email_confirmed_at, sign-in then succeeds, and the SHIPPED emailVerifiedFromUser answers true on the real /auth/v1/user body',\n  ngoTrip.followStatus >= 300 &&\n    ngoTrip.followStatus < 400 &&\n    ngoTrip.confirmedAfter !== null &&\n    ngoTrip.signInAfter.status === 200 &&\n    ngoTrip.accessToken !== null &&\n    ngoTrip.rawUserWire.status === 200 &&\n    ngoTrip.shippedVerdict === true,\n  `link follow HTTP ${ngoTrip.followStatus} -> ${ngoTrip.followLocation || '<no location header>'}` +\n    ` · email_confirmed_at before=${ngoTrip.confirmedBefore ?? 'NULL'} after=${ngoTrip.confirmedAfter ?? 'NULL'}` +\n    ` · sign-in after HTTP ${ngoTrip.signInAfter.status} (access token present: ${ngoTrip.accessToken !== null})` +\n    ` · /auth/v1/user HTTP ${ngoTrip.rawUserWire.status} ${redact(ngoTrip.rawUserWire.body)}` +\n    ` · the SHIPPED emailVerifiedFromUser says: ${ngoTrip.shippedVerdict}` +\n    ' · the extractor\\'s FALSE half is NOT exercised here: an unconfirmed user holds no session to read /auth/v1/user with',\n);\n\n/* =============================================================================================\n * (e) BOTH EMAIL-CAPABLE ACCOUNT TYPES FLOW END TO END UNDER THE FLIPPED CONFIG.\n *\n * WHY THE PREDECESSOR'S COMPLETION EVIDENCE IS NOT CITED HERE, which is gate-1 ruling [2] adopted\n * whole: the flip changes the auth context beneath BOTH completion paths. That evidence was\n * produced with confirmations OFF, so it predates this change and no longer covers it. Both paths\n * are therefore re-proved rather than referenced.\n *\n * The NGO address, already confirmed by (a)–(d), completes through the DEPLOYED complete-signup\n * edge function. The volunteer address repeats the whole round trip on its own, then gains a\n * GitHub identity by the operator insert above — flip-independent, because it is a database write\n * and not an auth flow — and completes as a volunteer through the same deployed function.\n * ============================================================================================= */\n\nconst ngoCompletion = ngoTrip.accessToken\n  ? await callFunction('complete-signup', ngoTrip.accessToken, {\n      accountType: 'ngo',\n      organizationName: 'Riverside Shelter',\n      acknowledgmentTextVersion: TEXT_VERSION,\n    })\n  : { status: 0, body: 'no access token — the NGO never signed in' };\n\nconst ngoRows = await sql`\n  select a.account_type, o.name, m.role\n    from public.accounts a\n    left join public.org_memberships m on m.account_id = a.id\n    left join public.organizations o on o.id = m.org_id\n   where a.id = ${ngoTrip.userId}::uuid`;\n\nconst volunteerEmail = address('volunteer-verify');\nconst volunteerTrip = await verificationRoundTrip(volunteerEmail);\n\nif (volunteerTrip.accessToken) await fabricateGithubIdentity(volunteerTrip.userId, VOLUNTEER_HANDLE);\n\nconst volunteerCompletion = volunteerTrip.accessToken\n  ? await callFunction('complete-signup', volunteerTrip.accessToken, {\n      accountType: 'volunteer',\n      acknowledgmentTextVersion: TEXT_VERSION,\n    })\n  : { status: 0, body: 'no access token — the volunteer never signed in' };\n\nconst volunteerAccountRows = await sql`select account_type from public.accounts where id = ${volunteerTrip.userId}::uuid`;\nconst volunteerProfileRows = await sql`\n  select github_handle, top_languages, repository_count, contribution_summary, imported_at\n    from public.volunteer_profiles where account_id = ${volunteerTrip.userId}::uuid`;\nconst expectedStats = stubGithubStatsFor(VOLUNTEER_HANDLE);\nconst volunteerProfile = volunteerProfileRows[0] as\n  | { github_handle: string; top_languages: string[]; repository_count: number; contribution_summary: string; imported_at: Date }\n  | undefined;\n\n// THE VOLUNTEER'S PRE-CONFIRMATION REFUSAL GETS THE SAME TEETH AS (c)'s, per the same ruling: a\n// claim that the volunteer \"repeats (a)-(d)\" is only true if it is measured the same way.\nconst volunteerRefusal = unconfirmedSignInRefusal(volunteerTrip.signInBefore);\n\nrecord(\n  'e',\n  'both email-capable types flow end to end under the flipped config: the NGO completes after confirmation, and the volunteer confirms, links GitHub and completes with its profile populated',\n  // The NGO half.\n  ngoCompletion.status === 200 &&\n    ngoRows.length === 1 &&\n    ngoRows[0].account_type === 'ngo' &&\n    ngoRows[0].name === 'Riverside Shelter' &&\n    ngoRows[0].role === 'admin' &&\n    // The volunteer half: its OWN round trip repeated (a)-(d), then completion.\n    volunteerTrip.signupCarriedSession === false &&\n    volunteerTrip.confirmedBefore === null &&\n    // THE REFUSAL IS THE CONFIRMATION ONE, not merely a 4xx — the same predicate (c) uses.\n    volunteerRefusal.ok &&\n    // AND THE LINK CAME FROM THE CATCHER. Without this, the admin-minted fallback could carry the\n    // volunteer half while the claim \"repeats (b)\" quietly narrowed to something smaller. The NGO\n    // half already cannot do that, because (b) fails with a NARROWED note on the fallback.\n    volunteerTrip.linkSource === 'emailed' &&\n    volunteerTrip.confirmedAfter !== null &&\n    volunteerTrip.shippedVerdict === true &&\n    volunteerCompletion.status === 200 &&\n    volunteerAccountRows.length === 1 &&\n    volunteerAccountRows[0].account_type === 'volunteer' &&\n    volunteerProfileRows.length === 1 &&\n    volunteerProfile?.github_handle === VOLUNTEER_HANDLE &&\n    JSON.stringify(volunteerProfile?.top_languages) === JSON.stringify(expectedStats.topLanguages) &&\n    Number(volunteerProfile?.repository_count) === expectedStats.repositoryCount &&\n    volunteerProfile?.contribution_summary === expectedStats.contributionSummary,\n  `NGO completion HTTP ${ngoCompletion.status} ${redact(ngoCompletion.body)} · rows=${JSON.stringify(ngoRows)}\\n        ` +\n    `volunteer round trip: signup carried a session=${volunteerTrip.signupCarriedSession}` +\n    ` · confirmed before=${volunteerTrip.confirmedBefore ?? 'NULL'} after=${volunteerTrip.confirmedAfter ?? 'NULL'}` +\n    ` · sign-in before confirmation ${volunteerRefusal.discriminator}, redacted body ${redact(volunteerTrip.signInBefore.body)}` +\n    ` · link source ${volunteerTrip.linkSource} (${volunteerTrip.linkEvidence})` +\n    ` · its own tampered-token probe: attempted=${volunteerTrip.tampered.attempted}` +\n    ` email_confirmed_at afterwards=${volunteerTrip.tampered.confirmedAfter ?? 'NULL'}` +\n    ` · shipped emailVerifiedFromUser says ${volunteerTrip.shippedVerdict}\\n        ` +\n    `volunteer completion HTTP ${volunteerCompletion.status} ${redact(volunteerCompletion.body)}` +\n    ` · account=${JSON.stringify(volunteerAccountRows)} · stored profile=${JSON.stringify(volunteerProfileRows)}` +\n    ` · the shipped stub's judgement for \"${VOLUNTEER_HANDLE}\"=${JSON.stringify(expectedStats)}`,\n);\n\n/* =============================================================================================\n * (f) THE MAILER RATE LIMIT — reported either way, so a starved run can never look like a clean\n *     one.\n *\n * `[auth.rate_limit] email_sent` caps auth emails per hour, and this run sends exactly TWO","lineStart":1,"lineEnd":989,"totalLines":990,"truncated":true}},"title":"loop\\items\\AI4DEV-59\\proof-local.ts","time":{"start":1786290395492,"end":1786290395506}},"id":"prt_fe7345902001mJi2VwxJAhJleD","sessionID":"ses_018cbdef8ffeIiDUqdR6dh2IlP","messageID":"msg_fe73445270019yfmL1VZxizM0z"}}
